===== editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/completion/whole-arc-read.md =====
     1	# Book 6 completion pass — Step 2: whole-arc read
     2	
     3	Reader: Codex, independent fresh-context seat. Date: 2026-10-11.
     4	
     5	Read before the manuscript: Book 5's whole-arc read; `EDITION_BRIEF.md`; all of `OWNER-DECISIONS.md`, with special attention to #33 and #35–#46; Book 6's `BOOK_MAP.md`, all nine AFTER MOVEMENT blocks in `STATE_LEDGER.md`, `protected-patterns.txt`, and `COMPLETION-PLAN.md`; Book 5 ch59–60; and Book 7's entry plan. Then read `manuscript/chapter-01.md` through `chapter-60.md` in order, every word, keeping `state/completion/whole-arc-notes.md` during the read. Mechanical audits followed: the calendar and locked counts; titles, names, possessions, injuries and quotations; all 51 patterns in `protected-patterns.txt` plus BOOK_MAP §8's Tier A/B units; repetition counts; `formula_metrics.py`; and the required source probe. No manuscript file was modified and no git command was run.
     6	
     7	## Verdict
     8	
     9	The book holds as a book. Its argument develops rather than merely accumulating: Cael begins with a true belief that text has protected him when people failed (ch7:225); the sittings demonstrate that honest people can fill every office and still produce a predetermined result (ch13:129–229; ch20:165–213); the road cut turns institutional pressure into a literal hand with no paper and no face (ch30:189–239; ch31:191–259); Seln's case proves that the instrument was designed to turn perception into a narrower official truth (ch35:195–293; ch36:83–173; ch39:91–143); and the ruling finally makes the system, not any individual villain, the acting hand (ch51:173–215, :255–263). The ending does not retract the first pole. It places the equally true second pole beside it—work within the system was real, and exclusion was designed—and then carries both past the jurisdiction rather than pretending departure defeats the machine (ch60:255–315). The last line lands because the book has spent sixty chapters separating self-knowledge from an office's power to name (ch60:343–371).
    10	
    11	The entry and exit seams, calendar, locked figures, fragment accounting, knowledge releases, quoted callbacks and Reader Standard all hold. Movements 5–9 are fuller by ruling and mostly earn that fullness: the road cut, the opening of Seln's case, the closing, the ruling, the council, the last bout and the withdrawal each need the page room they receive. The overlength is local, not architectural: re-briefing around the confluence (ch23–25), repeated object-and-silence rituals in ch44–47, multiple successive emotional endings after the last bout (ch54:164–312), and the long procession of individual exits in ch56.
    12	
    13	There is one literal continuity conflict, and it is protected. The minute says certification was “declined by the evaluator of record” (ch49:201), while the same scene identifies Gault as evaluator of record and says he declined nothing (ch49:209); the requested and declining certifying office is the Archmarshal's office (ch46:259; ch50:119–127; ch58:59–69). Because ch49:201 is a Tier B unit, this pass cannot propose or make a correction. The surrounding prose clearly notices the contradiction, so this may be intended as an institution's false form-label rather than an authorial slip; either way, it needs a coordinator ruling before the book can be called contradiction-free. No rebuild is needed. No non-protected line fix is justified. One same-author texture pass, in the two assigned lanes, should thin the extremely audible gesture and pause repertoire and take modest weight from the four local stretches above.
    14	
    15	## 1. Continuity
    16	
    17	### Calendar and deadlines — holds
    18	
    19	Every explicit day, interval and deadline agrees with BOOK_MAP §6 and the ratified table. The challenge is dated on the first day, served on the eighth, and due on the twenty-second (ch7:89–101). Lira's rated trial is day 30 and the advancement docket day 31 (ch12:3, :237); the first sitting is fixed on day 40 (ch13:91); the Archmarshal's refusal is day 44 and the later Velmere envelope arrives on day 45, fourteen days after the first letter (ch14:106–118; ch16:103–109). The fourth-sitting departure rule is correctly converted into the clean door from day 75 through day 115 (ch38:165–203). The confluence postponement and the default's forty-day count both remain consistent when carried from M4 into the later schedule (ch21:93–97, :223–255; ch23:171–173).
    20	
    21	The finish table is exact. The last Fifth-day carries Brom's panel and the ruling on day 201 (ch51:129–177). The council follows that night and crosses midnight into day 202; Ephram asks for the next-day bout there, and the bout is fought on the Sixth-day (ch52:259–341; ch53:215; ch54:1–160). The four withdrawals are entered on the Seventh-day, day 203, and the party leaves at dusk (ch55:3–169; ch56:307–341). Seln's empty desk and rendezvous are the First-day, day 204 (ch57:3–47; ch58:113–231). The final waystation departure is the Fourth-day, day 207, explicitly the last day of the academic year (ch59:105–107). They sleep at the last station, cross the Line the next morning, camp under the cart without fire, and reach the shelf on the following Sixth-day, “the second day past the Line” (ch59:231–305, :351–395; ch60:35–57, :191–201). Thus the shelf is the second camp past the Line and the first fire beyond it, as the governing seam ruling requires.
    22	
    23	Season-blindness holds. There are no season names used as calendar labels, no month-of-year order, and no English weekday names. The fixed date “the eleventh of Sowing” is the allowed exception (ch34:21), and “the year's last month” is the allowed landmark rather than a numbered month (ch46:259). Halcenvane's First-day through Seventh-day system remains internally consistent through the close (ch55:3–91; ch57:5–31; ch58:5–17; ch60:199–201).
    24	
    25	### Counts and figures — holds
    26	
    27	- The lane remains a bill, not a flag: ten counts slow, clean at eleven; a third close use costs twelve, clean at thirteen (ch4:191–215, :269; ch11:153–211; ch37:31). Lira ten / Force ten / Blade twelve is stated and demonstrated without later drift (ch11:153–195).
    28	- The file carries the grant and two renewals on paper in every non-protected count (ch8:137–159; ch16:123–135; ch20:169–173; ch26:103; ch55:69–75). Only the protected first pole says “a stamp renewed four times,” at its original and recalled placements (ch7:225; ch60:259).
    29	- Anchor perception is about ten feet, faint at twelve, absent at fourteen (ch38:341–353). The woman's fifteen-foot position in the road cut is a physical scene distance, not a competing perception limit (ch31:201).
    30	- Anchor is the eighth confirmed fragment (ch32:25–40, :116); Blade is the ninth (ch54:145–152, :264–266). The closing inventory names nine and keeps session nine/Tide-adjacent outside the count as one anomaly (ch60:203–243).
    31	- The clean door is the day-75 to day-115 withdrawal window, not the uncommanded Anchor doorway event (ch33:181–215; ch38:165–203). “Twice more” is granted at two and remains two when the inventory is revised (ch37:97–107, :258–262).
    32	- Hesk's clock loses in its normal direction from the first page through packing and the Line (ch1:7; ch46:119; ch48:141, :211; ch55:239; ch59:333–347). Calling it “right” beyond the last clock is a local convention explicitly explained, not a mechanical repair (ch59:333–347).
    33	
    34	### Titles and offices — one protected conflict
    35	
    36	Gault is evaluator of record from the first weekly sheet through his closing note (ch2:145; ch27:43–49; ch49:209; ch55:185–191). The Archmarshal's office is the certifying office: the seat requests its certification (ch46:253–259), Karis's supplemental filing calls it “the certifying office” (ch50:119–127), and the closing summary names the Archmarshal's office in exactly that capacity (ch58:59–69). The challenge and all later instruments address `Caelen Hesk-ward` (ch5:65; ch10:113–119; ch20:127; ch55:71; ch58:69).
    37	
    38	The exception is the protected minute at ch49:201: `Evaluation-protocol certification: declined by the evaluator of record; proceeding on the presiding seat's authority.` Gault, the evaluator of record, “had declined nothing” (ch49:209). The actual declining office is the Archmarshal's. The text dramatizes the bad label—the four evaluators stop writing, and the form has no line for why (ch49:203–213)—but it still contradicts the binding office names literally. Reported for coordinator ruling; not offered as a line fix because the sentence is Tier B.
    39	
    40	### Quotations, names, bodies, possessions and locations — holds
    41	
    42	Quoted callbacks were checked against first occurrence. The first pole is exact at ch7:225 and ch60:259. `I keep looking for the person and finding the hand` is exact at ch13:229 and ch51:263. `They're trying to move him back inside the instrument` is exact at ch7:137 and ch39:181. Jent's `None, Advocate…` is exact at ch26:95 and ch49:71; ch44:79 deliberately quotes only the identifying first words. Seln's good-room line is exact at ch34:105 and ch54:304. His catechism supplies `doors open both ways` at ch29:75, and Rooke's departure line pays it without wording drift at ch56:293. The Hesk letters are intact at ch19:87 and ch34:185; Daeva's statement and rematch language remain intact through ch59:377 and ch60:251. No accidental quotation drift was found.
    43	
    44	Names, pronouns, ranks and bodily sides remain stable. Lira's road injury is the right ankle; Brom's is the left shoulder; Karis's is the right hand; Cael's are left forearm and right ribs, and the road accounting records them together (ch31:109–133; ch32:50–122). Gault's role, Seln's Bronze grade and fifteen years, Karis's Ember / Iron Rank Three, Brom's Copper formal status, and Lira's Iron standing do not wander (ch37:31–37; ch52:131–169; ch55:47–59, :147–159; ch57:47). Hesk's book and clock remain Cael's; the sealed Velmere envelope remains Brom's; the locked case and its duplicate leaves remain Seln's; Karis's archive leaves in three boxes (ch34:51–63; ch35:153–195; ch52:115–171; ch56:131–165, :185–241). No person is placed in two scenes at once and no owned object changes hands without an on-page transfer.
    45	
    46	### Seams — holds
    47	
    48	Book 5 closes with seven confirmed fragments plus the uncounted anomaly, two renewals on paper, Hesk's clock losing again, Brom's review inside ninety days, two returning watchers, and Seln at the table (B5 ch60:151–173, :189–209). Book 6 opens about two months later with the same clock direction, the same seven-plus-anomaly count, the two watchers and the unresolved review (ch1:5–33). At the far end, ch60 gives five travellers, nine confirmed plus the anomaly, Shadow still sealed, three boxes and Seln's case under one canvas, `pending jurisdiction`, `active non-compliance`, and the first fire at the second camp past the Line (ch60:35–57, :191–243, :289–321). That is the state Book 7 needs. The older `first camp` / `left in form` shorthand in the Book 7 planning file is superseded by the governing #46 ruling and is not a manuscript finding.
    49	
    50	## 2. Knowledge boundaries — holds
    51	
    52	Seln learns neither Shadow's mechanism nor the reason for its seal before M6. Before the case opens he knows only his own service practice, the monitoring routes and that Cael has withheld something from the returns (ch18:161–217; ch19:107–137; ch22:91–149; ch29:43–103). In M6 he first opens the case (ch35:153–191); the group reads the station readings, entry orders, margins, reports and advisory before interpreting them (ch35:195–293; ch36:3–173); only then does Cael disclose the fragments and Shadow's source (ch37:1–178). Seln releases the debt but does not demand use: Shadow remains sealed while its use can be seen by anyone who could carry the seeing west, and otherwise belongs to Cael without further permission (ch37:188–208). Past the Line, Cael keeps it shut even on the first apparently unwatched night, and the closing inventory records both Seln's knowledge and the unchanged seal (ch59:351–359; ch60:205–219).
    53	
    54	The Kindling mechanics arrive in the right order and at the right altitude. Earlier scenes expose only what Cael could have seen at Denvash: the pale circle, the plate and the eleven-second non-result (ch9:147; ch13:117–121). Seln's leaves then separate the person's Arbiter waking from the station plate and official reading; keeper margins preserve what the frame perceived; the corrections function appends a narrower entry; and in Cael's case it fails safe because there is no destination column (ch35:195–217; ch36:83–123; ch39:29–67). The brass plate, source reading and correction are not collapsed into one machine.
    55	
    56	The Velmere day-45 envelope stays sealed on Brom's table. It arrives fourteen days after the earlier letter that was opened (ch12:255–275; ch16:103–109), remains whole through the sittings and road (ch19:71; ch20:127; ch22:47–63; ch29:217–249; ch32:50–65), and is left unopened beside the unsigned finding (ch56:145–161). The two pieces of correspondence remain distinguishable.
    57	
    58	Reserved truths are not spent. The papers establish a policy about two hundred years old, an older `Unbound` entry, and a corrections function, but Karis expressly limits what the case cannot tell them: maker, underlying nature, true age and origin (ch39:91–143). The abandoned station adds an older sigil stone, an unfinished open register line and a frame that goes quiet for Cael, but names no Architect, Quieting, source civilisation or Tide practitioner (ch59:187–229, :265–275; ch60:289–321). The anomaly stays uncounted and under `Still open. Still real. Patience.` (ch37:37; ch60:211). The faceless Anchor faction remains unnamed and unlocated. Daeva, Reydan, Vastin, Gault, Rooke, Withrow and Ephram know no more than their scenes permit.
    59	
    60	## 3. Book-wide repetition — the worst ten
    61	
    62	Counts below are case-insensitive phrase-family occurrences across the sixty manuscript files. They deliberately include some literal or character-specific uses: the number defines the pool a human pass should inspect, not an instruction to delete every match.
    63	
    64	1. **Pause/time staging — 202 hits, all 60 chapters.** `for a moment / while / time`, `for some time`, and `for the space/length of` have become default breath marks: Cael sits with a fact “for a while” (ch4:245), the satchel is studied “for a long while” (ch34:51), Jent stands “for a moment” before speaking (ch49:177), and Seln studies one line “for some time” (ch57:211). The pauses often carry real weight; their ubiquity makes the weight predictable.
    65	2. **Flat — 200 hits, 57 chapters.** Some are technical (`flat hand`, ch3:149; ch31:81), but the adjective also repeatedly supplies voice, pose and surface (`hands flat`, ch41:193; ch44:41; ch56:197). The technical uses should remain; generic hands/voices/surfaces need a harder cull.
    66	3. **Square / squared / squaring — 188 hits, 49 chapters.** Square hands and capitals are useful signatures (ch3:205; ch17:121), but paper, files, cases and piles are squared to edges with near-ritual frequency (ch17:193–211; ch44:179, :237; ch56:101). By M8 a reader expects the object to be squared before the room goes quiet.
    67	4. **Negative looking — 171 hits, 55 chapters.** `did not look`, `without looking`, `never looked`, and `not looking` repeatedly carry withheld feeling: the officer checks Cael and looks away (ch5:43), Seln works without looking up (ch18:69–79), the Anchor woman is not looking at Cael (ch31:25), Brom asks about roads without looking at the table (ch42:53). The first instances are precise; the book-wide accumulation becomes a tag habit.
    68	5. **Silence formulas — 142 hits, 51 chapters.** `nobody/no one said, spoke or answered`, `said nothing`, and `did not say anything` recur after documents and gestures: Brom's silence as agreement (ch4:15), the room after Hesk's letter (ch19:89), the birthday table (ch34:149), Lira after the closing (ch49:251). Silence is one of the book's subjects, but the same grammatical declaration over-labels it.
    69	6. **Characteristic “the way…” gloss — 121 hits, 43 chapters.** A gesture is often followed by an explanation of how that person always does it: Cael reports in the order Karis wants (ch5:101), Brom treats furniture in his careful way (ch28:151), Lira opens and shuts her hands “the way she did” when pleased (ch43:53), Karis writes in the way she always does (ch28:245). These glosses can make observation feel pre-interpreted.
    70	7. **Negative asking — 75 hits, 33 chapters.** `did not ask`, `nobody asked`, `without asking` becomes the book's second silence tag: the group agrees not to ask Brom (ch16:117), Cael does not ask what is in Seln's case and nobody asks later (ch29:129, :233), the assistant is not asking what the lamp meant (ch55:219). Several are plot-bearing consent boundaries; many smaller ones repeat the same emotional notation.
    71	8. **`not X, but Y` recast — 70 hits, 39 chapters.** The construction repeatedly corrects the reader inside the sentence: Karis calls verification “not caution but manners” (ch28:101); an answer comes “not because they mean to, but because” it is natural (ch40:43); Vastin bows “not to any of them, but to the room” (ch40:153); the Current watches “not at Lira's face… but at her feet” (ch43:69). It is clear and forceful, but too often the book's preferred interpretive turn.
    72	9. **Man/woman/person explanatory similes — 39 hits, 27 chapters.** People look or move “as a man/person” doing a second, illustrative act: the officer checks Cael like a man checking a sum (ch5:43), the green instrument is held like something found alive on a doorstep (ch5:153), Brom drives a shoulder like a man driving a post (ch31:129), the Current reads feet as a person reads a repeated sum (ch43:69). The device is good at physical clarity and overused as emotional explanation.
    73	10. **Quarter-turns — 25 hits, 18 chapters.** Rules, cups and bodies turn by a quarter with metronomic precision: the counsel's rule at the third sitting (ch20:189), again at the last closing (ch44:69), Seln's cup on the road (ch58:269). This began as a useful object motif and has become a general gesture available to too many characters.
    74	
    75	The motifs to protect from indiscriminate cleanup are Hesk's losing clock; Brom's pockets, loads and first-count-right recounts; Seln's cup, case and exact office hands; Bracken's clocks, glasses and square capitals; Gault's straight case; literal floor geometry; and technical flat-hand / quarter-angle fight language. The problem is not any one image. It is the same small physical vocabulary doing emotional work for nearly everyone.
    76	
    77	## 4. Arc and pacing
    78	
    79	### The argument
    80	
    81	M1 establishes the strongest possible version of “work within the system.” The clause has held; the house answers; every dangerous provision is real law; Cael's receipts are real receipts (ch5:65–77; ch7:55–79, :121–145, :225). M2 does not simply repeat the hope: the first sitting shows the honest seat constituting itself and listening, while the hooded room converts human judgement into position and procedure (ch13:129–229). M3 then separates honest officers from institutional outcome. Vastin's true assessment cannot enter the proceeding (ch14:98–148); Gault, Umber and Withrow can place honest limits in the record without changing the machine's direction (ch17:159–241). M4 completes the first half's turn when the petition admits it does not turn on evidence (ch26:65–115).
    82	
    83	M5 is the midpoint and the title's physical proof. Paper provenance and the missing seal converge (ch28:19–47; ch29:3–39; ch30:13–87), then the road cut reveals custody without paper, face or blade (ch30:189–239). Cael acquires the system's own holding craft and turns it backward (ch31:191–259); the next sitting lets Seln name the road hand as the same hierarchy he was taught in office (ch32:258–268). M6 changes the question from “who corrupted the instrument?” to “what was the instrument built to do?” The answer is not a villain but a corrections function maintained through ordinary forms (ch35:195–293; ch36:83–173; ch39:29–143). Brom's line at ch39:201 and Cael's answer at ch51:263 close the book's search for a single person.
    84	
    85	M7 turns knowledge into choice. The party decides not to spend the cache in a way that exposes Seln or forty children, while still attending the proceeding so the institution must write what it does (ch41:135–215; ch42:185–229). M8 pays both halves: Karis makes the best case the room can hold (ch48:183–225; ch49:117–175); the ruling goes around it rather than through it (ch51:173–223); the council chooses the clean door with every cost on the table (ch52:1–171, :205–341); and the last bout gives consent, earnestness and the ninth fragment a form the system unexpectedly describes truthfully (ch53:99–215; ch54:5–160, :240–266). M9 individualizes the cost of leaving, then shows the apparatus continuing beyond their departure (ch55:3–167; ch56:95–281; ch57:43–227; ch58:45–101; ch60:289–321). The argument lands where the plan says.
    86	
    87	### Where fullness earns its length
    88	
    89	- **M5, ch30–32:** fully earned. The road fight is not spectacle; each exchange teaches subtraction, fixed points, accumulation and the moving stitch, then accounts for the bill on every body (ch31:23–227; ch32:50–122). Cutting the teaching sequence would weaken both the fragment and the institutional metaphor.
    90	- **M6, ch35–40:** fully earned. The case has to be read before it is theorized, Shadow has to be disclosed after the evidence rather than before it, and Vastin's borrowed room needs stillness for his refusal to matter (ch35:153–293; ch36:3–173; ch37:1–208; ch40:115–207). This is the book's explanatory centre and remains dramatic because every abstraction is attached to an object or a choice.
    91	- **M7, ch41–43:** earned. The closing's bones, the cost of publication, the orchard walk and Lira's return to the floor translate the revelation into decisions (ch41:135–215; ch42:37–229; ch43:3–93). The material does new work rather than recapping the cache.
    92	- **M8, ch48–53:** earned through the end of the bout's first movement. Karis's fifty-one minutes, the protected certification blank, the ruling, the council and Ephram's request are separate turns with separate prices (ch48:183–225; ch49:117–215; ch51:173–223; ch52:1–171, :271–341; ch53:99–215).
    93	- **M9's individual departures:** earned in conception. Lira leaves with usable instruction, Brom leaves the finding and sealed family letter, Karis turns standing into an archive, Seln leaves a perfectly accurate empty record, and Cael inventories the road (ch56:17–91, :131–165, :185–281; ch57:43–227; ch60:117–163, :191–275). They are not interchangeable farewells.
    94	
    95	### Where fullness reads as padding
    96	
    97	- **Ch23–25:** the confluence meet itself is distinct and the one-point bout pays it, but the approach re-explains sealed lines, ladders, files and what a public floor proves after those ideas are already secure (ch23:41–123, :171–209; ch24:193–233; ch25:19–77). A texture pass can remove 500–700 words of anticipatory and aftermath explanation without touching the bout, the protected `Again. Faster.` line or the production order.
    98	- **Ch44–47:** good scenes recur in the same ritual: a paper or rule is placed, squared or quarter-turned; the room is silent; a plain sentence explains what the silence means (ch44:69–79, :177–179, :237; ch45:227; ch46:253–259; ch47:179–221). The content advances, but the staging makes four chapters feel more alike than their events are.
    99	- **Ch54:164–312:** the bout finishes, then Gault's response, the crowd, Withrow leaving, the Current girl, Ephram's handshake and speech, the Log, the ninth's interpretation, Bracken's forms and the good-room callback each supply another ending. Nearly every beat is good; together they defer release after the decisive action. Keep the protected notice, speech and Log entry, and shorten connective reaction around them.
   100	- **Ch56:** the four-leaf climax has already happened in ch55. Ch56 then gives Lira's last class, Rooke, Seln's counter, Brom's packing, all three boxes, the district officer, Ephram, Withrow, Rooke again, the watchers, ferry and guesting-house Log (ch56:1–341). The sequence is emotionally true but accumulative. Two or three explanatory callbacks can go while every departure image remains.
   101	
   102	The book does not rush anywhere that damages comprehension. Ch26–27 accelerates deliberately from the fourth sitting to the Anchor watcher (ch26:193–221; ch27:43–129), and ch55's quick withdrawal forms are earned by the long preparation in ch52–53. The shelf sequence has enough road and logistics before the final fire (ch58:305–355; ch59:3–163, :279–395; ch60:3–57).
   103	
   104	## 5. Protected lines — complete, with one protected/canon conflict
   105	
   106	All 51 non-comment patterns in `protected-patterns.txt` matched under its normalization rules. BOOK_MAP §8's additional Tier A and Tier B units were checked on the page. Every required occurrence is present once at its mapped place, character-for-character; deliberate mapped quotations and callbacks account for the few phrases that recur.
   107	
   108	**Tier A.** Anchor notice ch32:25–40; Blade notice ch54:145–152; `I knew the system was imperfect…` ch40:197; `active non-compliance…` ch60:315; the title attestation at its mapped instruments and callbacks (ch5:73–77; ch6:75; ch7:235; ch26:201; ch51:179, :255); `Still open. Still real. Patience.` ch37:37 and closing anomaly ch60:211; `Not this either. Still open.` ch39:167; Hesk's two letters ch19:87 and ch34:185; the limit and middle/beginning lines ch39:139–143; `pending jurisdiction` ch58:51, :69 and ch60:301; the borrowed office ch40:97–115; the unnamed woman ch37:35 and ch38:245; Daeva's statement and reply chain ch18:105, ch20:121, ch59:377; the first pole ch7:225 and its required recall ch60:259; both-poles line ch60:269; Seln's absent-desk opening ch57:3; `You kept my file too` ch37:182; four files in order ch56:271; Withrow's record line ch53:79; and `the hand with no face` ch34:229 / ch60:207.
   109	
   110	**Tier B.** The filing note ch5:193; challenge first page ch5:65–73; chapter fourteen and Withrow's consequence ch7:55–79; corridor ch7:121; `choosing` ch11:39; Karis's parenthetical ch13:253; constituted-seat sentence and tab ch14:106, :134; benched instrument ch15:153; `None, Advocate` ch26:95; `Again. Faster.` ch24:221; escort order ch26:201; Seln's four-line catechism and truth exchange ch29:73–79, :131–135; eighth entry ch32:116; good room ch34:105; map ch39:243; ruling paragraph ch51:177; boxes and resignation ch52:161–167, :243; Ephram's speech and ninth entry ch54:240–266; Gault's final note ch55:185–191; personnel query ch57:47; waystation board ch59:77; frame address and thanks ch59:271; `Nobody's watching` ch59:323; first look east ch59:493; rematch letter ch59:377; and the closing exchange ch60:343–357.
   111	
