   113	
   114	## 6. Reader Standard — met, with one clarity caveat
   115	
   116	A thirteen-year-old can follow the plot and the book's central reasoning in one pass. Legal abstractions are repeatedly made physical: a corridor with one-way doors (ch7:121), six slips across a table (ch7:107–129), a map before the first sitting (ch13:69–87), names and a rule turned by quarters (ch20:165–189), a clean door with dates on it (ch38:165–203), a scale and two instruments at the close (ch49:117–215), and three roads on three slips after the ruling (ch52:13–63). The densest action—the Anchor cage and moving stitch—is demonstrated through bodies and road points before it is named (ch31:151–227). The densest history ends with Karis explicitly stating what the evidence cannot prove (ch39:91–143).
   117	
   118	Violence is consequential but not lurid. The road cut records ankle, shoulder, hand, forearm and ribs with first-aid and recovery rather than gore (ch31:109–133; ch32:50–122). The last bout's shoulder injury stops the action and is treated (ch54:121–160). There is no sexual content, exploitative romance, slur, or profanity issue. Coercive institutions and surveillance are named as wrong by the people who experience them, and the book never invites the reader to admire the nameless custody team.
   119	
   120	The one clarity caveat is the protected certification line. A careful young reader is told in the same scene both that “the evaluator of record” declined and that Gault is that evaluator and declined nothing (ch49:201–209). The later phrase `certifying office` helps (ch50:123; ch58:61), and the contradiction may be intentional proof that the form lies, but the page does not explicitly say “the form has named the wrong office.” This is the same coordinator issue as §1, not a separate line fix.
   121	
   122	The earlier opened Velmere letter and the day-45 sealed envelope are close enough to invite a momentary identity question, but the prose resolves it by dating the second arrival fourteen days later and thereafter keeping its red seal visible (ch12:255–275; ch16:103–109; ch19:71; ch56:145–161). It does not fail the standard.
   123	
   124	## 7. Rhythm outliers
   125	
   126	Whole-book meter: 296,518 words; 21,766 sentences; sentence mean 13.62, median 10; 28.5% at five words or fewer; 4.0% at forty or more; paragraph mean 38.95, median 27; 254 marked scene breaks; 944 words per scene; Flesch reading ease 89.1; FK grade 4.21. The scene figure is almost exactly the 950-word working target. The book is syntactically easier than the formula proxy, which suits the Reader Standard.
   127	
   128	- **Ch57 is the clearest long-metre outlier:** 5,089 words; sentence mean 16.69, median 15; paragraph mean 46.69, median 45. The extended Seln retrospective and report-voice make the long paragraphs purposeful (ch57:43–89, :95–157, :203–227), but this is the one chapter where the book's pulse visibly changes.
   129	- **Ch14 is the second long-metre outlier:** 3,504 words; sentence mean 16.53, median 12.5; paragraph mean 50.78, median 38; 6.1% of sentences at forty words or more. Vastin's inward, institutional reading justifies the slower syntax (ch14:45–148). No meter-only cut is needed.
   130	- **Ch22, ch26 and ch30** also run longer than the norm at means 15.69, 15.21 and 15.62; ch26 has 6.7% of sentences at forty-plus words. They are council, sitting and road-setup chapters, and remain readable (ch22:41–63, :91–149; ch26:65–115, :193–221; ch30:13–87, :171–239).
   131	- **Ch33, ch52 and ch59 are clipped outliers:** means/medians 12.12/7, 12.20/9 and 12.43/8.5. Ch33's recovery experiments, ch52's five-person council, and ch59's road inventory use lists and dialogue, so the clip is functional (ch33:25–215; ch52:13–171; ch59:3–163, :231–395).
   132	- **Length alone:** ch59 is longest at 7,980 words and ch53 next at 7,440. Both have seven effective scene units and roughly 1,000 words per scene; neither is a structural outlier. Ch56 feels longer than its measure because it chains exits rather than because its sentence meter departs.
   133	
   134	Verdict on rhythm: no chapter requires a meter repair. The texture pass should address repeated staging, not force every chapter toward the mean.
   135	
   136	## 8. Line fixes
   137	
   138	None authorized.
   139	
   140	The only exact continuity correction indicated by the read would touch ch49:201, a Tier B protected unit. Per the protected-text rule, no `old/new` replacement is proposed. The coordinator must first rule whether the line is (a) intentional in-world form error, in which case the manuscript should remain untouched, or (b) a protected wording defect, in which case the protected unit must be formally released before an exact fix is supplied. All non-protected candidate concerns resolved on audit; therefore there is no string to list merely to fill the section.
   141	
   142	## 9. Three cross-movement priorities for one same-author texture pass
   143	
   144	1. **Thin the shared object/pose vocabulary without erasing character motifs.** Search the 188 square-family and 200 `flat` hits. Lane A (ch1–30): revise or remove about **45** generic uses—especially papers squared to edges, non-technical flat hands/voices and square descriptions that do not belong to Bracken, Gault or Seln. Lane B (ch31–60): revise or remove about **55**, concentrated in ch41–47 and ch55–57. Replace with action specific to the current object, or omit the gesture and let the spoken line stand. Leave alone literal geometry, `flat hand` fight technique, Bracken's square capitals, Seln's office hand, Gault's straight case, and every protected run.
   145	2. **Stop declaring every pause, silence and withheld question.** The audit pool is 202 pause phrases, 142 silence formulas, 171 negative-looking constructions and 75 negative-asking constructions. Lane A: recast or cut **50–60** combined instances, with special attention to ch7, ch19 and ch23–25. Lane B: recast or cut **60–70**, especially ch34, ch41–49 and ch54–57. Use one of three alternatives: end on the object or line without explaining the room; give the next character a concrete task; or let a short spoken response replace the narrated silence. Preserve plot-bearing consent boundaries (the decision not to ask Brom, Seln's Shadow terms, Ephram explicitly asking for the bout), and preserve silence that is a named character's established language rather than generic scene punctuation.
   146	3. **Vary the scene-ending machine and take 1,200–1,800 words of duplicate explanation, not event.** The recurring machine is: object placed or quarter-turned → collective stillness → `not X but Y` or `the way…` interpretive sentence → plain aphoristic close. Lane A: break **six to eight** such endings and remove **500–700 words** of re-briefing in ch23–25; keep the confluence bout, production order, all required file beats and protected lines. Lane B: break **eight to ten** endings and remove **700–1,100 words** around ch44–47, ch54:164–312 and ch56; keep the protected ruling, notices, Ephram speech, Log entries, four walk-out images, three boxes and first-fire sequence. Alternative endings should land on a changed task, spatial movement, an unanswered concrete fact or immediate consequence—not another maxim. Do not alter scene order or add new material.
   147	
