     1	# Chapter 59 — The Registry Line
     2	
     3	He kept a list, that week, of what the road still had.
     4	
     5	He did not put it in the book. It was only a column in his head, and each day it was shorter than the day before. On the first day it had run to a dozen things: metalled stone underfoot, ditches, dressed milestones with the district's device, a keeper at the ford with a stamp, a chartered sign, a board of hours. On the second day there had been a post bag on a hook, sealed in lead, and a notice of tolls in the registry's print, and a rider in district grey going west at a trot, who had not looked at them.
     6	
     7	They slept the second night at a waystation with a long room and a stove. Its keeper was a narrow man who counted out hay for the gelding and the cob behind them, and then named a figure, and Brom went out to the rack with a lantern and came back and laid down exactly half of it.
     8	
     9	"Four nets," he said. "I counted them on the rack and I counted them in the manger. You've said eight."
    10	
    11	The keeper looked at the coins, and at the size of the man who had put them there. "It's eight for the two beasts," he said.
    12	
    13	"The other beast's rider pays for his own. I check." Brom did not raise his voice. He went back out to the yard with the lantern, and he was gone some time.
    14	
    15	Cael found him there afterward, crouched beside the brown cob, with its near fore foot between his knees and his awl in his teeth. The grey rider was nowhere to be seen, having taken his supper to the loft, and the cob stood for it patiently. A shoe had been clicking on that foot since the second ford. Cael had heard it behind them all afternoon, a small loose sound, and had thought nothing of it.
    16	
    17	Brom set the two nails that had started, and clenched them over with the back of the whetstone. He put the foot down and ran his hand along the leg, and stood up.
    18	
    19	"He'd have been lame by the top of tomorrow's hill," he said.
    20	
    21	"He's following us."
    22	
    23	"The horse isn't." Brom wiped his hands on his coat. "The horse is only walking where he's pointed. I'll not listen to that for two more days."
    24	
    25	He said nothing about it to anybody, and neither did Cael, and in the morning the cob went sound.
    26	
    27	The third day was the hill.
    28	
    29	The woman with the good horses had called it a long climb, and she had not been making it sound worse than it was. The road left the river's country an hour after light and went up, not steeply but steadily, round the shoulders of the land, for the whole of a day. The gelding stopped at the first shoulder, as a clock stops: he planted all four feet and blew, and nothing Lira said to him made the least difference.
    30	
    31	"She said one a day," said Lira. "She didn't say how much of the day."
    32	
    33	"Take the weight off him," said Seln. "Not the load. The people."
    34	
    35	So they walked beside the cart, all of them, and on the worst pitches Brom and Seln put their backs to the tailboard, and Cael put his right shoulder there, low, where it did not have to lift. Karis carried a wedge of ash from the crossroads fire, and each time the gelding stopped she had it under the wheel before the cart could run back a hand's breadth. She did it forty times before noon, and her face was grey with it, and she would not give the wedge to anyone.
    36	
    37	"They're mine," she said, when Brom put out his hand. "I can't carry them. I can at least stop them rolling."
    38	
    39	It was on that hill that the list grew short.
    40	
    41	The metalling gave out first, and he marked the place: a last yard of broken stone rammed flat, and after it the road was earth, rutted, with grass down its middle. The ditches ended a little higher. Then the milestones changed. *Forty-one* was dressed, and had the river and the scale cut over its number; *forty-two* was a rough upright of the hill's own rock, with a figure daubed on it in white; and there was no forty-three, though he looked for it for an hour. At a place where a track came in there was an iron post with a hook on it, and nothing on the hook.
    42	
    43	"Every second day," said Karis, breathing hard. She had stopped beside it with her wedge. "I said so, and I was guessing. It may be every tenth, up here. It may be when somebody remembers." She touched the bare hook with one pale finger. "I wonder who decided. Somebody sat at a desk and decided that past this post a letter wasn't worth a horse."
    44	
    45	"Somebody added it up," said Brom. "It'll have been right."
    46	
    47	"I'm sure it was right. That's what I mind."
    48	
    49	Behind them, three hundred yards down the slope, the grey rider had got off his cob and was leading it. He kept his distance on foot exactly as he had kept it mounted, and when they stopped, he stopped, and let the horse crop.
    50	
    51	They came to the top in the last of the light. The land lay over on its back there, wide and bare and rolling, with the wind going across it, and half an hour on stood a low building with a lamp in its window.
    52	
    53	---
    54	
    55	It was the last chartered waystation, and it had the sign to prove it.
    56	
    57	The board hung from an iron arm over the door, with the registry's seal burned into it, and the wind had swung it against the wall so often that it had worn a pale groove in the stone. There was a yard with a wall round it, and a well with a lid, and a stack of cut turf against the gable, higher than a man.
    58	
    59	The keeper came out with a lantern before they had the gelding out of the shafts.
    60	
    61	He was old, and bent a little to one side, as a thorn bends that has had the wind on it all its life. He held the lantern up and looked at the horse first, and then at the cart. He walked round to the tailboard and lifted the canvas's edge with two fingers, as a man looks under a cloth at a market, and let it drop again.
    62	
    63	"You've boxes," he said.
    64	
    65	Nobody answered him, for it had not sounded like a question.
    66	
    67	"Carters have bales," said the keeper. "I've had carters forty years. Bales and sacks, and a sheet I put my mark on. Folk who are running have bundles, and I get three or four of those a year: a blanket with the corners tied, and they won't meet my eye, and they're gone before I've the fire up." He scratched his jaw with the lantern's ring. "I don't get boxes. Roped, with a knot like that. Somebody took time over those, and you don't take time if you're running."
    68	
    69	"We're not running," said Lira. "We're walking."
    70	
    71	"I can see that. That's what I said." He turned toward the door. "Hay's what it says on the wall, and I don't charge it twice, whatever you've heard lower down. Mind the step. It's worn."
    72	
    73	The room was low, and warm, and smelled of turf. It had six beds along one side, and a table, and on the wall beside the door a board in a frame.
    74	
    75	Cael stopped in front of it, and Karis was there before him. It was the registry's own print, under glass, with the seal at its head, and it was the last such board on that road. They had passed one at every waystation since Ostrand, and he had read each of them in passing, as he read everything; he read this one through, standing, with his arm aching in its strap.
    76	
    77	*By charter of the registry, this house being chartered: TIER VERIFICATION available at the district station, Ostrand, four days west. No station lies east of the river crossing, and the station at the ford is unstaffed. TRAVEL ENDORSEMENTS may be lodged at this house for forward transmission; allow twenty days. ENROLLMENT INQUIRIES by the same. The chartered rates of this house are posted within. Complaints of a chartered house lie to the district seat.*
    78	
    79	"Twenty days," said Karis. She had her finger on the glass. "For an endorsement."
    80	
    81	"So it says." The keeper had hung his lantern on its nail. "I've never seen one come back in under thirty. It goes down that hill in a bag, when there's a bag, and somebody looks at it, and it comes up again. A man could walk to the district and back in the time, and some have." He lowered himself onto the bench by the fire. "Mostly they don't wait. They read that, and they stand where you're standing, and work it out, and then they go on east without, or they go home."
    82	
    83	"Who stamps the carters' sheets?" said Lira. "They told me the stamps ran as far as the station. That's a day on."
    84	
    85	"They run as far as my drawer." He nodded at the table. "There's been no keeper at the station these four years. They took the man and left me his brass, and a letter to say I was to mark for him until further instruction, and I've had no further instruction. So a sheet says *station*, and it means an old man at a turf fire a day short of it." He looked into the fire. "Nobody's complained. There's nobody past here to complain to."
    86	
    87	Seln had not come to the board. He stood inside the door with his satchel on him, and he was reading the room, not the wall; Cael saw his eyes go to the drawer in the table, and stay there a breath, and move on.
    88	
    89	There was another paper on the wall, beyond the board.
    90	
    91	It was not under glass. It had been pinned up bare, a long time ago, and had gone brown at its edges. Cael knew the print, and he knew the sheet, for it was the registry's public notice of tiers, which he had seen on the wall of every station and guild hall he had ever stood in, and in an instrument-maker's shop, behind the door. There were five words down its left side in heavy type, Copper, Iron, Bronze, Silver and Gold, and against each stood its ranks, and the fee for an evaluation, and the office to which a practitioner should apply.
    92	
    93	Somebody had done a sum on it.
    94	
    95	It was in pencil, in the broad margin beside *Silver*, and it ran half the way down the sheet. *Nets, 14. At 2 the net. Less 3 owed from the grey mare.* A line was ruled under it, and a total, and the total had been crossed out and corrected. Whoever had done it had wanted a piece of paper with room on it, and had found one.
    96	
    97	Brom came and stood at Cael's shoulder. He read the notice down, from *Copper* to *Gold*, and he was a long while at the second word. Then he read the sum.
    98	
    99	"That's right," he said. "The second total. He'd carried a one wrong the first time, and he found it." He looked a moment longer. "It's the most use I've ever seen that notice put to."
   100	
   101	The keeper laughed, a dry sound, without turning from the fire.
   102	
   103	---
   104	
   105	They left him at first light on the Fourth-day.
   106	
   107	It was the last day of the academic year. Cael had known it when he woke, as one knows a date that has been set against one's name for a long time. Somewhere behind him, six switchbacks up from a river, a bell would ring that evening and the house would close its books; and at some hour between, on a north road short of a toll-bar, a stout fair man would unlock a windowless building. He did not know the hour, for Withrow's sheet had given only the day, and he kept looking at the sun.
   108	
   109	"You've gone quiet," said Lira, at the horse's head.
   110	
   111	"They'll open it today. The frame-house. It's booked."
   112	
   113	"For you."
   114	
   115	"For the book's sake, he said. He'll have to stand about." He shifted the strap on his arm. "I keep thinking I ought to feel it. A door being held open with my name on it, and nobody walking through."
   116	
   117	"And do you?"
   118	
   119	He thought about it honestly.
   120	
   121	"No. I feel the hill in my legs."
   122	
