    93	
    94	## 3. The expansion plan — where the extra ≈197,000 words come from
    95	
    96	Nothing below may change an end state, move a death, add an acquisition, or disclose a reserved truth early (§11). Every new episode must fit the sequence locks in §13. Word figures are planning estimates, not quotas.
    97	
    98	### A. Fights and set pieces given developed room (≈46,000)
    99	
   100	| Fight / set piece | Source | Edition allowance | Why |
   101	|---|---|---|---|
   102	| **The cut** (stillhounds; Iron Wall's first showing) | S4, ~1,800 | 3,500–4,000 plus a scouting scene | Iron Wall's limit must be taught here (rooted, loud, the man is the plane's foot) before the alley pays it off. The crew's silent doctrine is re-taught to a man who has only ever stood. |
   103	| **The alley** (the stepped cut, six faceless) | S12, ~2,400 | 4,000 | The corridor rewards the wall the road punished. Cael learns "being the reason" from inside it. The acquisition minute needs its long, quiet middle. |
   104	| **The Span** (watch-post; barricade; width) | S19, ~2,600 | 4,500 | Force must be witnessed for a full exchange. The width tactic is invented live and fairly priced: nothing is gained inside the line. |
   105	| **The pass** (rig; seven volleys; the death) | S21, ~3,800 | 5,500 | This is the book's costliest fight. The terrain causes two beats (the fall, the swept scree). The young one's death must be prepared and not gratuitous. Force's first use is paid in the ribs. |
   106	| **NEW — The shale-back herd at the axe-head fence** (winter) | summarized as "we walked the fence" | 3,000 | A winter set piece needing no Path: a herd of armoured grazers sheltering on the fence line's slope must be moved without stampeding them ("lethal on a slope when herded wrong"). It teaches the Iron read on frozen ground, Lira benched by her sling, Seln's posts, and Brom reading a line. |
   107	| **NEW — The ice road** (161st) | one line | 3,000 | A carter's cart goes through the river-ice. Cael, Brom and Seln haul it out without declaring, reading load on the ice. The fragments are taught as practical knowledge. It pays later at the cut's frozen runnel. |
   108	| **NEW — The silent yard** (winter learning bouts with Teague's crew) | absent | 3 bouts × ~1,500 = 4,500 | No Paths, steel and bodies only, in the frozen meadow. Cael loses to the tall woman's spear reach (she never speaks), then corrects. One-armed Lira coaches from the bench. The rival clock appears on the page. This must not touch the sequence locks in §13 (no wall drill; Seln teaches nobody). |
   109	| **NEW — Lira's return to the floor** (270th–276th) | absent | 2,000 | Her first two-armed bout since the Ring, against Cael. The arm "tells her" once and she does not stop. That is a visible correction, and it pays at Millrace ("Once."). |
   110	| **NEW — The ferry's first landing** (277th, high water) | quiet landing | 1,500 | Rope and flood. Vastin is seen holding a physical line, with no Path, before anyone knows it is him. Iron Wall must not show before the cut. |
   111	| **NEW — The tail in Treswick** (291st–292nd) | absent | 2,500 | After the alley, Seln and Cael tail a hood through the outer district at night. The hood passes through a tier gate Cael cannot legally cross. The faction stays faceless and unlocated, and the promise and the bar become physical. |
   112	| **NEW — Water at the seep** (301st–302nd, dry camps) | "two camps with no water" | 3,000 | The two crews must water themselves and a mule at a seep where shale-backs stand on a slope. Teague's field captaincy is shown. The young one moves too fast and the tall woman stops him with a hand. That is his habit, set up for the pass. |
   113	| **Lesser exchanges given their beats** | — | ~4,500 | The alley's aftermath; the Span's broad-man fall; the ridge recovery of the mule; the court's pacing as a physical task. |
   114	
   115	### B. Learning and training arcs (≈30,000)
   116	
   117	1. **Cael's working inventory in winter** (≈6,000; first third). Through private practice in the ferryman's shed and the frozen meadow, the reader learns all ten fragments as costs and limits. Wind's burst and its locked landing beat. Pressure's narrow channel. The Iron read as "the workhorse". Compression's frame. Ember, never used. Shadow's idle draw and the seal's logic. Storm asleep. Anchor used both ways. Blade, short and sharp. Tide "ask first", which he cannot practise on anyone and does not read on himself (Oryn said stop). **Invent no new numeric ceilings.** Costs are qualitative unless canon gives a number.
   118	2. **The kit and the cord** (≈3,000). Karis teaches the crew to pace with the cord. The anomaly is discovered **as a scene** on the 168th, not as a log line.
   119	3. **Vastin learning the crew's doctrine and "to be nobody"** (≈5,000, distributed). Day one of the border road is a silent-movement drill: walk silent, signal by hand, nobody declares unless meant to be loud. Then the cut's lesson, the spring's "Draft nothing", the yard door's "Be nobody", his week of moving toward the lamp and stopping (four times, then two), and the first order he takes and keeps (Millrace).
   120	4. **The Deepstacks decipherment** (≈8,000). The chamber days 285–292 are put on the page, including the undramatized 288th–289th. Cleon's method (bones before words), the board's numerals, Karis's letters coming one at a time from the three floors, and the lamp's discipline. Karis refuses to guess. **The leaf must not become readable on the page before the seventh day** (§13).
   121	5. **Rune practice and cost** (≈3,000). After the 291st Cael catches himself reading made things everywhere: the inn table's joints, the bridge's arches, the dividers' hinge. He learns that the eyes pay, and pays it in the dark (Millrace). It is never used on a person. He does not turn it on himself until it turns unbidden.
   122	6. **Force, dry** (≈2,000). On the shale step (305th) he lines his frame without firing. The thirteenth "listens" and the ribs warn him. The first actual use comes at the pass.
   123	7. **Lira's arm** (≈3,000). The rehab runs from the release to Millrace's "an arm": grip work, the floor-testing rise, the left hand into the coat and out to check it moves, and two arms used on the scree at the cut.
   124	
   125	### C. Summarized events put on the page (≈46,000)
   126	
   127	- **The winter months (131st–264th)** (≈14,000). The first week of ice. The pricing scene at the inn. The board in winter. The fence trip. The river becoming a road. Hesk and Vell's letters gone west on the last ferry, with the answers pending. Karis's back room. The long dark. The thaw. Pike in his room. The source's log entries become scenes, and the log stays as punctuation between them.
   128	- **The week before the ferry (270th–276th)** (≈4,000). Tents return to the meadow. Teague's crew prepares for spring. Oryn's first Lowmarch stop: patients at the plank, seen from the outside.
   129	- **Treswick's days** (≈8,000). The outer district as a mechanism: brokers' tally boards, the smith's two forges, the ring road at night. The undramatized chamber days.
   130	- **The 294th: the list of parts** (≈2,000). Copying all day, Cleon's finger on the last line, and his correction "Your hand's better than mine on the fourth line".

