     1	# Chapter 1 — The Valley With No Name
     2	
     3	*Second day past the Line. One fire. Deployment since the Line: none.*
     4	
     5	He had written that under the inventory an hour ago. The last line of it was his own count and nobody else's: it was for the nine things he carried, and whether he had let one of them out. Since then he had done nothing at all but keep it true.
     6	
     7	The fire had burned down to a red eye, and Cael let it go, for Brom had said at the start of the watch that a man who feeds a fire all night ends by looking at the fire, and Brom was generally right about anything that had weight. So he sat with his back to the embers and his face to the dark, on a shelf of turf that nobody had ever troubled to name, and he counted.
     8	
     9	It was an old habit, and he had it from the hill he had left a week ago, where somebody had always been set to watch him. For a year there had been two men at the foot of a road with a tin cup between them, and a third on a tier with a pencil, and a mild clerk at a copying table who had turned out to be the best of the four; and he had learned to find each of them in the first minute of any morning, as a man feels for his purse. He ran the count now over a country he could not see. He took the ridge behind them first, and the scree, and the black shoulder to the north where a night bird kept its two notes, and then the gulf in front, in sections, from the near ground outward.
    10	
    11	Nobody was there.
    12	
    13	He counted again, since the first answer had been such a strange one, and the second came out the same. The habit did not know what to do with nothing, and it went round a third time without being asked, like a dog that has been called off and has not understood.
    14	
    15	"Zero," said Brom.
    16	
    17	He sat at the shelf's lip, ten paces off, with his back to what was left of the light, and he had not turned his head.
    18	
    19	"You can't hear a man count."
    20	
    21	"I can hear you stop breathing at the end of each one." Brom spoke low, as he had been taught in an orchard. "That's three. It's zero. I've checked."
    22	
    23	"How many times?"
    24	
