    28	
    29	He had no Path of his own, and so no tier and no rank, and he said so to himself plainly, because the dark was a good place for plain things. Everybody else he knew had come away from a Kindling at fourteen with one: a single gift, which an Arbiter named and a registry ranked ever after. His own Arbiter had woken too, on a brass plate in a windowless room, and it had taken eleven seconds over him and gone dark, and it had been dark ever since. What answered him now were nine fragments, which was the log's word for them: abilities taken out of other people's Paths, each one inside a fight that somebody else had begun in earnest, and each one written into Hesk's book with a name beside it and a price.
    30	
    31	They were not quiet, either, though he had supposed, coming east, that they would lie still on a road where nobody was asking him for anything.
    32	
    33	Shadow, the quiet one, had lifted at dusk, as it lifted at every dusk, soft behind all the rest, and he had held it where it was while Seln laid a fire ten feet away. Blade was the newest of the nine and the quickest, and it had asked about the edge of the scree, and for once had been told that it was right. Storm did nothing whatever. It was a Gold's worth of weather, the heaviest fragment he owned, and it sat in him as a full pail sits on a step, so that he was aware of it all day in the way that one is aware of a pail.
    34	
    35	None of that was use. He had spent two years keeping such things down under other people's eyes, and he had let none of them out since a ring of churned turf beside a split thorn, from a caution that he could not yet have given a reason for. But he had begun to see that carrying nine things and not using them was a kind of work, and that it went on all night.
    36	
