   137	They walked without talking, and by the middle of the morning Cael found that he had stopped missing it. Lira's long knife and Brom's bill rode where a stranger would see them, and at noon they sat on a bank and ate cold cake, and not one of the five so much as glanced at the dead wood lying under the trees.
   138	
   139	The steading stood in a loop of the river, behind a fence of split posts as high as a man's chest, and the old man who held it was at his gate before they had come within a bowshot. He had a white dog beside him, and a face like the bark of the red trees. He read Pike's mark on the slip, and then he looked at their hands, each pair in turn, and it was the only question he put to them.
   140	
   141	"Forty," he said, and walked them along the fence to where a stretch of it had gone at the butts and leaned outward like a row of drunkards. "Cut them in the coppice. A stride apart, and deep. There's an axe." He turned back toward the house, and the dog stayed.
   142	
   143	It stayed with Brom, who cut for six hours that day, in a stand of straight grey poles above the river, with the old man's axe and the hedging bill, and whenever he stopped to wipe his face the white dog walked over and sat down on his feet. He told it twice that it was in the way, and after that he worked round it.
   144	
   145	Cael watched him for a part of the first hour, while he and Lira were getting the measure of a spade and an iron bar. The axe was an old one, and its edge had been ground a little out of true, so that every tenth stroke or so it glanced in the grain and kicked back up the helve into the hands that held it. That was a load shifting, with no warning at all, and each time it happened Cael looked at Brom's wrists, and each time they were as brown as the rest of him.
   146	
   147	"You're counting," said Brom, not turning round.
   148	
   149	"Eleven kicks. No grey."
   150	
   151	"Twelve. I had one while you were looking at the spade." He set his feet and swung. "It's not that I'm stopping it. I've found the place just before it starts, that's all, and I stand there. It's a narrow place. I'd not like to have to find it in a hurry."
   152	
   153	He kept the count of his posts on a length of cord, a knot to each, hung over a branch. In the middle of the afternoon a goat came up out of the river meadow and ate the cord to the last knot but three, and Brom, who had the number perfectly well in his head, laid down the axe and went along the stack and counted every post over again with his finger.
   154	
   155	"I check," he said to the goat, which was unmoved.
   156	
   157	Lira dug. She detested it, and said so at every hole, and was better at it than Cael by a long way, since she had a fighter's sense of where to put her weight. By evening they had eighteen posts in and the old fence drawn.
   158	
   159	It was the new butts that Karis was looking at. She had read about fences, as she had read about most things, and she told Cael the principle while she chose a post from the stack. The end of a post that goes into the ground is charred first, to the depth of a finger, because rot will not take hold in charcoal; a fencer does it in a fire, and turns the post as he would turn a spit. She laid her post across two stones with its butt clear of the grass.
   160	
   161	"This isn't a road," she said. She did not make it a question, quite.
   162	
   163	"No."
   164	
   165	"And there was a fire in his yard when we came. I saw the smoke of it." She put out her left hand, with the pale fingers spread, and reached toward the cut end of the wood.
   166	
   167	"Not at my fence."
   168	
   169	The old man had come along the line without a sound. He stood six feet behind her with his thumbs in his belt, and he was not angry, any more than a man is angry who tells a child that a pan is hot.
   170	
   171	"There's a fire in the yard," he said. "Use the brands."
   172	
   173	He went on down the fence, pushing at each new post with the flat of his palm as he passed it.
   174	
   175	Karis had not moved. Her hand was still out, over the wood, and her mouth had come a little open; and Cael, who had sat beside her through fifty-one minutes in front of a hooded seat, watched her want something more than she had wanted to win that day. He saw her mouth close, and her hand come back into her lap.
   176	
   177	She took out her notebook, and he read it over her arm, since she tilted the page for him.
   178	
   179	*Twenty-second day. At the steading on the north water. Asked not to light at the fence line; directed to char with brands from the yard fire. A fire in a yard is permitted. A point at a fence is not. Therefore the rule is not a rule about fire.*
   180	
   181	Under that she wrote one line more, slowly, and ruled beneath it.
   182	
   183	*I did not ask him why. Entered: the not-asking.*
   184	
   185	"He would have told you," said Cael.
   186	
   187	"I think he would. I should have had a whole sentence from a primary source, and it's the one sentence I want in the world." Her voice was quite steady, and her knuckles were white on the pencil. "But the courier asked us nothing, and Pike asked you your age and stopped, and nobody in that meadow has yet asked me what's in my boxes. I believe that asking is a thing paper does. I should like to find out what one learns the other way."
   188	
   189	"And what have you learned?"
   190	
   191	"So far," said Karis, "one fact about a fence and one about myself. I'm not at all sure which I'd sooner have had."
   192	
   193	She got up and went to fetch the brands.
   194	
   195	---
   196	
   197	They set the last post on the second afternoon, and the old man walked the whole of the line.
   198	
   199	He was a long while about it, for he put his palm against every post of the forty and leaned, and the dog went with him and sat down each time he stopped. At the end he stood with his back to them and looked along the tops, where Brom had sighted each one against the last with a length of string. Then he went up to the house.
   200	
   201	He came back with four coins in a sock and a boy behind him carrying a sack on his shoulder. The coins he counted into Brom's hand. The sack, which was flour, and a good twenty pounds of it, the boy let down at Brom's feet as though that were the natural place for anything heavy. The old man scratched his mark on the slip with a nail, three upright strokes and a bar across them, and gave it back.
   202	
   203	He looked along his fence once more, and then at the five of them.
   204	
   205	"Cheap," he said.
   206	
   207	He shut his gate, and they heard the bar go across.
   208	
   209	"He means us," said Lira, on the road. She had gone a hundred yards with her jaw set. "*Cheap.* I dug twenty-two holes in that man's ground."
   210	
   211	"He means the work came to more than he paid for it," said Brom. He had the sack on one shoulder and the bill in his other hand, and he was walking more lightly than a man should with twenty pounds of flour. "He was pleased. He'd no other word for it."
   212	
   213	"Then it's a complaint. Against himself."
   214	
   215	"It may be. I'll take it, though." Brom walked a little way. "I've been told what I was worth by a district panel and a provincial counter and a letter with a red seal on it. That's the first time anybody ever said it and thought he'd had the better of the bargain."
   216	
   217	They had started late, and dusk caught them four miles short of the meadow.
   218	
   219	Cael felt it coming this time. The sun went behind the western ridge, all at once, as it did; the river turned from brass to lead; and he had dug holes for two days and his shoulders ached to the bone. It lifted behind the rest of what he carried, soft, and offered him the old thing. He had the refusal ready before it finished asking.
   220	
   221	His left boot came down on the road in silence.
   222	
   223	It was one stride only, for he had it shut before the right foot landed, and the gravel spoke up again as rudely as ever.
   224	
   225	"Left boot," he said aloud. "One stride."
   226	
   227	"I heard it." Seln was a little ahead, on the verge, and his voice was hardly more than breath. "I was waiting to learn whether you would tell me."
   228	
   229	"I report everything. You know that. You read it for two years."
   230	
   231	"I did. It was a great deal of true paper, and I burned my copy of every sheet." He seemed to turn something over. "One stride is better than four. I should expect none at all by the time the weather turns, if you are as obstinate as I was. It never quite stops asking."
   232	
   233	"Does it ask you?"
   234	
   235	"Every dusk of my life. I tell it that I am walking home."
   236	
   237	Brom made bread that night, with the old man's flour, on a flat stone at the edge of their fire. He had seen it done, he said, and it could not be hard. The cakes came off the stone black on both faces and white in the middle, and Lira weighed one in her hand and asked whether the smith had been consulted.
   238	
   239	"It's what the board paid," said Brom, with dignity.
   240	
   241	They ate every one.
   242	
   243	*Twenty-fourth day. Second line: forty posts, cut and set. Four marks and flour. He said "Cheap," and Brom has kept the word. Earned, six. At dusk the sealed one took my left foot for a stride, on an empty road. Rent. Deployment since the Line: none.*
   244	
   245	---
   246	
   247	Seln took the cold road on the thirtieth morning, and that was the last of the five.
   248	
   249	He had walked the east road on the ninth evening, within an hour of stepping off a ferry, as far as a saddle and a little beyond it, and the north before light on the tenth. The south road had cost him two mornings, since it forked. The fourth was the road they had all come in by, and he had gone back along that one too, three miles, to see it as a person sees it who is arriving. This was the fifth. It left the north road at a dead tree and climbed, and the meadow called it cold; he learned why at the first turn, where a wind came down off bare ground that had no business in such mild weather.
   250	
   251	He went up it in the grey, alone, at the pace he had once taught four young people in an orchard.
   252	
   253	Nobody had sent him, and he was clear with himself about that, as he tried to be about most things. For fifteen years somebody had always sent him, and there had been a line on a sheet to say where, and a second line for the hour at which he was to be back. When he left the last of those houses he had expected that particular habit to be among the first to go.
   254	
   255	It had not gone. He walked, and at each turn of the track he did the three things that he had always done: he found the place where a man would stand who wished to watch the road without being seen from it, and the place where the road was narrowest, and he looked for water, and paced it from where a camp might lie.
   256	
   257	Then, since he could not help it, he wrote it up.
   258	
   259	He did not use paper, for he had not put pencil to it for that purpose since a crossroads, where he had lit a fire with the last one. But the old form ran in him like a tune, and as he climbed it set itself out in the voice he had kept for the audit. *Cold road: leaves the north road at the dead tree, eleven hundred paces above the last roof. A mule's width from the outset. Water at the second turn, forty paces off the track, to the left. Three places of concealment below the crest, none showing use.*
   260	
   261	None showing use. He stopped at the third of them, a notch between two boulders, and looked at the grass in it.
   262	
   263	It was long, and it stood straight, and he had found the same at every one of them, on five roads, for twenty days. A man who stands in a place, morning after morning, to watch a road leaves the mark of his feet there, and of his waiting, and Seln knew that mark as a baker knows his own thumb in a loaf. He had left it himself in every town he was ever posted to. There was none of it here. The grass had never been stood in, and he did not think that there was, in all this country, one person whose work it was to watch another and write down what he did.
   264	
   265	He had not known that he would find that restful. He stood in the notch himself for a moment, from habit, and then he stepped out of it again, feeling a little foolish.
   266	
   267	*Nothing further to report*, the form said, and came to its foot, and waited to be folded.
   268	
   269	That was the part he could not get used to, for a return was a thing with a desk at the far end of it. He had made seven hundred of them, more or less, and at the end of each there had been somebody who asked for it, in a level voice, across a table; and he had given what could be checked and kept back all the rest, so evenly and for so long that it had been mistaken for a manner of writing. He had five roads in his head now, entire, down to the paces, and they were better than anything he had ever sent in, for he had left nothing out of them.
   270	
   271	And nobody had asked.
   272	
   273	He did not offer it. That was not sullenness; he had examined himself for it, with some care. He told them whatever bore on the day in hand: he had given them the gauge of the east road when a horse depended on it, and he had put five people where they ought to stand on a boat. The rest he kept, as he had always kept the rest, and he went about with it and waited for the level voice. *What did you see?* It would come at a fire, he supposed. The boy would say it, since the boy wrote everything down.
   274	
   275	The boy had not said it; on the first night in the meadow he had looked at the dust on a pair of boots, and then he had very deliberately looked somewhere else. Seln had been watched for most of his life and had watched for the remainder. He could not remember being left a thing to keep before, and he had not yet settled how such a courtesy ought to be entered.
   276	
   277	The case knocked against his hip. He turned at the crest, where the wind was, and went down.
   278	
   279	The sun was on the river when he came out above the meadow. Four people were on the gravel bar. One of them ran, and stopped dead on a line, on her two feet, and flung up both arms; and a large one carrying a tree let it roll from his shoulder and stepped out from under it as neatly as a dancer.
   280	
   281	There was an alder on the bank that would have covered him. He stood clear of it, in the open, with his hands where they could be seen, until the large one looked up and found him and raised an arm; and then he went down to them with five roads in his head, and found that he was still waiting, and did not altogether mind.
   282	
   283	---
   284	
   285	A boy brought the slip in at first light on the thirty-third day, on a mule that had been made to trot.
   286	
   287	Pike read it twice, and did not tack it up; he passed it to them over the oak. *Wagon in the wash, east road. Out before the water. Three marks.* There was a mark under the words, a square with a peg at each corner, like a hide stretched to dry.
   288	
   289	"Before what water?" said Lira.
   290	
   291	Pike pointed with his pen. Over the eastern ridge the sky had gone the colour of a plum, in a stack, and under it hung grey curtains that were rain falling on somebody else.
   292	
   293	They were at the wash in three hours, walking fast and saying nothing, with a coil of the ferryman's rope across Brom's chest.
   294	
   295	It was a dry stream cut deep through red earth, twelve feet down and thirty across at the bottom, with banks too steep to stand on. The road went into it by a ramp dug out of the eastern side and climbed out by another on the west. A trickle ran in the bed, no wider than a hand. Karis looked at that trickle before she looked at anything else, and went down and drove a peeled stick into the gravel at its edge, and cut a notch in the stick at the waterline with her knife.
   296	
   297	The wagon lay at the foot of the far ramp. It was a long four-wheeled thing, and it had come down from the east loaded with baled hides. Its near front wheel had found a boulder. The wheel was a hub and a handful of splinters, and the wagon had slewed off the ramp's foot and settled on that corner, canted over, with its tail up the slope behind it.
   298	
   299	The woman with the hides was on the western lip with her two mules. She had got a third of her load up the ramp by herself, a bale at a time, and she was grey to the hair with dust. Cael knew her, for she had once laid a barley cake on the shaft of their cart, and she had been standing ten feet away when an old man asked him what he was. She pointed at the bed of the wash with her whole hand, and that was her greeting.
   300	
   301	"Hides first," said Brom, very low. "Then it's a third the weight."
   302	
   303	They made a chain of it and had the bales out in half an hour, and while the last of them went up, Brom walked round the wagon as he walked round any load. When he came back he had the whole of it, and he gave it to them with his hands as much as his voice.
   304	
   305	A pole went under the broken corner, lashed fore and aft, so that the wagon would ride on three wheels and a skid. The long rope went from the tongue up the western ramp to the mules, and to Brom. That was the haul. But the bed fell away downstream, and a thing dragged across a slope will always swing its tail to the low side and go over. So a second line must run from the tail-ring back to the eastern bank, and somebody must stand there and pay it out, a foot at a time, and never let it go slack.
   306	
   307	"That's the light end," he said, and looked at Lira. "It wants somebody quick. There's a thorn stump on the lip. Two turns round it, and the stump holds the wagon. You only hold the stump."
   308	
   309	Lira walked down the bed and up the far ramp with the line over her shoulder.
   310	
   311	Cael had the wedge. It was a block of ash, split from a limb beside a crossroads fire, and he had carried it over a line and down a scree, and stopped a cart with it some hundreds of times. Seln had a pole to lever with, and they took a wheel each.
   312	
   313	"One notch," said Karis, from the gravel. "The water's brown."
   314	
   315	The mules leaned, and the wagon groaned and came a yard, grinding, and the skid bit and held, and Cael kicked the wedge in under the hind wheel. It came another yard, and on the eastern lip Lira let a foot of line go round the stump, and then another, with her whole body bent back against it, and the tail came round sweetly behind the nose.
   316	
   317	Then the skid rode up on a stone.
   318	
   319	The wagon lurched over toward its broken corner, and for the space of a breath it hung there, deciding. The check line came up out of the dust as taut as a bowstring. Lira went forward two strides on her heels, toward the edge, and Cael saw her knees bend in the way he had seen on the gravel bar, three strides short of a line.
   320	
   321	She did not go. She threw a third turn round the stump and sat back on it with all her weight.
   322	
   323	The wagon settled onto its skid again. Then, having been denied that way, it went the other: the rope up the ramp slackened and snatched, a mule lost its feet, and the whole dead weight of the thing came back down the haul rope into the one man who had it across his shoulders.
   324	
   325	Brom's boots went from under him.
   326	
   327	Cael saw the grey start at both wrists, and he saw it stop. Then Brom did the only thing that a very large man can do who has decided to be nothing more than that: he sat down. He sat down hard in the dirt of the ramp with the rope round his body and his heels dug in, and was dragged a yard on the seat of his breeches, and another foot, cutting two furrows; and there, like an anchor that has found bottom, he held.
   328	
   329	Something in Cael had risen to meet all of it. A fixed point set under that hind rim and told to stay would have settled the matter in a breath, and the one that knew such things had seen the rim and said so. *There?*
   330	
   331	*Wheel*, he told it. *Hers. Leave it.* He drove the ash under the iron with his heel, and Seln's pole came down beside it.
   332	
   333	"I've sat down," said Brom, through his teeth, from the ramp. "It's all I had."
   334	
   335	"Three notches," said Karis. "And there's a sound."
   336	
   337	It came from up the wash, low and steady, like a cart crossing a wooden bridge a long way off.
   338	
   339	The check line had fouled. Cael saw it as the mule found its feet again: a loop of it had dropped off the lip and caught behind a rock in the bed, twenty feet out from the eastern bank, and while it lay there Lira could neither pay out nor take in. She had seen it too, from above. It was twenty feet of open air to that rock, and she could have been standing on it in the time it takes to blink.
   340	
   341	She ran down the ramp instead, and went through the water, which was over her ankles now and the colour of the banks, and freed the loop with both hands, and ran back up the way she had come. It took her a count of twenty where a burst would have taken one. From any ridge in that country, nobody would have heard a thing.
   342	
   343	"Now," said Brom, and the mules leaned.
   344	
   345	The wagon went up the western ramp on three wheels and a pole, a yard and a wedge at a time. Its tailboard had cleared the lip by perhaps the length of the wagon when the wash filled, and the water did not come as a wave. It came as a brown tongue a foot high, quite quiet, with sticks in it, and behind the tongue the bed simply rose. Karis came up the ramp at a run with her skirts in her fists, and when Cael looked back from the top her notched stick was gone, and the place where a wagon had lain was hip deep and moving.
   346	
   347	The woman with the hides scratched her mark on the slip against her own tailboard. She had not spoken a word in three hours. She looked now at Brom, who was red earth from his heels to his belt, and at Lira's hands; and last she looked at Cael, for a good while, with her head a little on one side.
   348	
   349	"You're not paper," she said.
   350	
   351	It would have been easy to take, for he was tired, and it was meant kindly, and it was the best thing anybody had said to him since a registrar wrote in a margin. But a clerk had written him down at fourteen, in a district office at the far end of the country, and he had been in somebody's book every day since. One morning in a ditch had not unwritten it, and he did not want the first good opinion he earned out here to rest on a mistake.
   352	
   353	"I am," he said.
   354	
   355	She thought about that, and she neither took her own words back nor argued with his; and then she clicked her tongue twice at her mules.
   356	
   357	Lira came and stood by him while the hides went on again. Her palms were raw across the heel, and she was holding them a little away from her coat.
   358	
   359	"I said no at the first stride," she said. "And the second. I lost count somewhere past thirty."
   360	
   361	"And at the stump?"
   362	
   363	"That wasn't no. That was a rope." She turned her hands over and looked at them. "A turn of rope round a stump does what a burst can't, as it turns out. Somebody might have told me that at Fenmark."

