     1	# Chapter 15 — Ground
     2	
     3	The paper was on the oak before the mist had left the river, and Lira read it with one boot on and the other in her hand.
     4	
     5	She had gone up to the eave on purpose to be first. Cael came after her at his own pace, which on level grass was anybody's pace now, and he took his inventory on the way, as he took it at the start of anything. The calf was a leg again on the flat; he had asked it the evening before, on the saddle road, and it had told him nine strides uphill and then shut. Brom's forearm was closed along the whole of its seam and wrapped by Brom's own choice, and it was to pull nothing. Lira had wound a strip of linen round her right ankle, over the stocking, and she walked on the whole sole of that foot. Karis had two pink lines across each palm and held a pencil for a quarter of an hour at a time. The nine fragments lay in him as they had lain since the ford, idle, each with its rent paid.
     6	
     7	There were fifty-two marks in the purse, and the name at the top of the sheet was still up the north water on two holds' slips.
     8	
     9	"It's a mountain," said Lira.
    10	
    11	She did not turn round to say it. She stood before the left-hand post with her head a little on one side, reading a second time, and her lips moved at the figure.
    12	
    13	"It isn't a ford at all. The ford's only where they pay you." She put her boot on at last, hopping. "He let me say I'd had enough of fords, and he knew what was on it the whole time."
    14	
    15	"I told you to read it first," said Pike.
    16	
    17	He had come out with the book under his arm and his coat on, and he set the flat stone on the page before he so much as glanced at the new slip. He had read it by lamplight when the carter brought it, and had no need to look.
    18	
    19	It was real paper, which was rare on that plank, and the hand was a careful one that did not write often. Beside the words somebody had drawn a horned head, broad between the horns, over three wavy strokes. Cael knew the strokes. A carter had drawn every ford on a calfskin with those three lines, and Karis had them by heart.
    20	
    21	*The high traverse, Oxhollow to Thornwater. To be walked with a cart, and mapped. Paid at Oxhollow, on the map: seventy marks.*
    22	
    23	Pike read it out for the meadow, and then he read it again from the first word, with his shoulder turned to the tents. There were not many to hear. The dry roads had emptied the grass to seven fires, and at most of those people were still asleep.
    24	
    25	"Seventy," said Brom. He had come up behind them without a sound, as a large man can who has spent sixteen days with nothing in his hands. "That's more than the six lines we've got. All of them put together."
    26	
    27	"By eight," said Karis.
    28	
    29	"What's a high traverse?" said Lira. "In a carter's mouth. I know what it is on a hill."
    30	
    31	"It's the same thing with a wheel on it." Pike laid his one hand on the paper. "Oxhollow's six days north-west of this plank, and Thornwater's two days east of it. A cart from the one that wants grain from the other comes round by my ferry, and it's eight days, and eight home. There's high ground between the two holds. The salt crews say there's a shelf along the top of it, a bench, with a gorge down one side. A mule string went over it once, before I'd this book, and came down in three days." He looked at the horned head, twice. "Somebody's told the ford so. The ford wants to know whether it's true before it sends a wheel."
    32	
    33	"The ford," said Lira.
    34	
    35	"Oxhollow's a ford with a fence round it. The keeper takes a copper a wheel, the same as my ferryman, and it's the keeper's paper. He'd sooner his wheels came back."
    36	
    37	"And he pays at his end," said Karis. "He's left nothing in your box."
    38	
    39	"It's six days of road, and he'd not trust seventy marks to the inside of a carter's hat. I'd not, either. That's what *on the map* means. You put the map in his hand, and he puts seventy in yours, and his mark goes on that paper under my strokes. Then you bring the paper here, and I say it."
    40	
    41	Karis had her notebook out, and her pencil was already turning in her fingers.
    42	
    43	"What does he call a map?" she said. "I've a registry sheet in the middle box with the whole continent on it, printed, and a calfskin that a carter drew for his own wheels. They aren't the same kind of thing. Which does he want?"
    44	
    45	"He wants to know where a wheel goes and where it doesn't. He's a ford-keeper. He'll have no more letters than he needs to count a toll." Pike thought about it. "Draw it so a man can follow it with his finger. Put the numbers where he can find them if he's a mind to."
    46	
    47	"A strip, then, with the shelf drawn down the middle of it, and a table beside. A line to every stretch." She was writing as she spoke. "I can rule that tonight."
    48	
    49	Cael read the slip through once more, for the thing that was not in it. There was no name on it but the hold's. Nobody had asked to have it kept.
    50	
