    52	
    53	He went along the whole plank afterward, from the left-hand post to the right, as he had gone along it on the ninth day and on the forty-ninth.
    54	
    55	There were fourteen slips. He gave each the same attention, the bark and the hide and the two shingles, and he sorted them as he read, as he had once sorted a board in Ardenmere by what each card would cost him. There was a fence to be set for a steading on the north water, at five marks, and a wheel to be fetched for a carter on the south road, and hay to be got in at the upper inn for a mark and the loft to sleep in. A string of mules wanted walking westward to the turf fire, and that slip he passed over without stopping.
    56	
    57	When he had done, the paper with the horned head was at the top of the heap in his mind, and it had got there so quickly that he distrusted it. He made himself say why, in items, as he would have made a stranger say it.
    58	
    59	The first was the plainest, which was that it paid. Seventy marks was more than Pike's book had counted out to them in six lines, and the crew was still four hurts short of whole.
    60	
    61	The second was that nobody's finger was on it. The last large slip they took had carried a captain's name in capitals. A one-armed man had struck that name with his own pen before a meadow, and they had gone down to a river under the weight of it. This one had been posted for anyone at all, with no hold on it. If they took it they would take it off nobody, and the captain at the top of the sheet could read it when he came down and find nothing in it that had been his.
    62	
