    56	
    57	He had expected nothing else. But he understood, getting up, what she had asked of him at the slab two days before: a hand that might doubt a number on that table before a wheel was put on it. It was an odd use to be wanted for, by a person who had once kept an index of everything he could do, and he liked it better than any of the rest.
    58	
    59	They took her through an inch at a time. Seln knelt at the off wheel with his cheek nearly on the rock, watching the rim against a scratch that Karis had made, and lifted one finger for every hand's breadth of travel. Cael had the shafts alone, so that there should be one pair of feet to steer by. It took a quarter of an hour to go four yards, and the only sounds were the dry hub and five people breathing through their mouths.
    60	
    61	"Cart: yes," whispered Karis, when the tail was clear. "This cart, with a man at each wheel who has nothing else in his hands. And under that: *a wagon of five feet six between the hubs, no.* I want him to have the number where the yes stops."
