    90	
    91	Karis opened her mouth and shut it. She turned to Cael, as people turned to him when there was a ruling to be given.
    92	
    93	He had it ready, and it was not the one that she expected. On any floor he had ever stood on he would have decided this himself, and in a breath, since the plan was always his. But the thing in front of him was a weight and a gap, and whether a back could take the one over the other; and there was a person on that shelf who had known such sums since he was twelve years old, and it was not Cael.
    94	
    95	"It's a load," he said. "Loads are his. If he says it goes, it goes, and he tells us where to stand."
    96	
    97	Brom gave him a slow look, as if a thing had been set in his arms that he had not thought to be handed. Then he nodded, and began to give orders, very low.
    98	
    99	The boxes went first, passed across the crack from two pairs of hands to two more. He had the cart emptied to her boards. He folded his coat into a pad and laid it on the back of his neck, and he had Seln and Cael step over the gap and take a shaft each from the far side.
   100	
   101	"When I say, you lift the shafts to your shoulders, and you walk. Don't pull her. I'm bringing her. You're only keeping her nose up." He looked round. "She'll want to tip back when I've got her, and that tail-board'll swing."
   102	
   103	He did not say what was to be done about that. He crouched, and went in under the bed between the wheels, backward, until the axle-tree lay across his shoulders on the folded coat. He set his hands on his knees.
   104	
   105	"Now."
   106	
   107	Cael lifted. He felt the shaft come light in his hands, and then lighter, and he looked back along it and saw the wheels leave the rock.
   108	
   109	Brom had stood up under her.
   110	
   111	He had not straightened altogether; he was bent like a man beneath a low beam, and the cords of his neck stood out, and every part of the cart that was not on two shoulders at the front was on his. It was the whole of her. Cael watched the two wrists, on the two knees, and waited to see Iron Skin start at them, as he had seen the grey start at a ferry and a wash whenever a weight came on suddenly. Thirty animals were scraping on the hill above, and there was a page in Karis's coat that said what a start might be worth.
   112	
   113	The wrists stayed the colour of wrists.
   114	
   115	Brom took two short steps to the edge. Then he took one long one, with his left boot going out over two feet of nothing and coming down on the western lip, and the cart went over the crack on a man's back. The tail-board swung out on its chains as she tipped.
   116	
   117	It did not strike. Lira had a hand under it on the near side, and Karis had the back of her wrist against it on the off, and they walked it across between them and laid it against the frame as one shuts a door on a sleeping house. Nobody had told either of them to be there. He had been looking at Brom, and had not seen them move.
