   132	
   133	Beyond the eighth the mountain let go of the bench at last, and it opened like a hand.
   134	
   135	He had lived for five days on a shelf that he could have thrown a stone across. Now the wall on his left fell back in a long curve and came round again two hundred yards on, and between the wall and the drop there lay a bay forty yards deep, floored from side to side with fine loose shale that had come down the face and spread. The slope above it was the same roof-pitch of plates, but lower; the upper shelf ran across it not three hundred yards up. He did not need to count what was on that shelf, and he counted it all the same, and it came to thirty.
   136	
   137	Seln had a fire laid, and he was sitting beside it with his hands on his knees.
   138	
   139	He had chosen the eastern corner of the bay, hard against the eighth narrowing, where a slab leaned out from the wall and water ran down the rock behind it into a basin the size of a hat. The cart stood on sound stone there, and that was as well, for she would have floated on every yard of the bay's floor.
   140	
   141	"You'll have seen the rock," said Seln.
   142	
   143	Cael had. Nobody could have missed it. Two-thirds of the way along the bay a blade of pale stone stood up out of the shale, quite alone, halfway between the foot of the face and the edge. It lay square across the fall of the ground, like a wall across a field, and it was as long as a barn and no thicker than a garden wall. At either end it was no higher than a man's waist, and in the middle it was twice his height, and the evening sun lay along its upper side and left the lower black.
   144	
   145	"I walked round it this afternoon. The shale is heaped against the upper side of it to the height of my chest, and it has gone round both ends, as water goes round a pier. On the lower side there is bare floor, a piece as large as this camp, with nothing lying on it at all." Seln turned his hands over to warm the backs. "That has not happened once. A slide has come down this face and gone round that rock a good many times, and never yet filled the place behind it. I should have slept there, if it had water. I do not care to carry a skin seventy yards across loose stone in the dark."
   146	
