   148	
   149	Cael put it in the catalogue, with its length and its height and the bare floor on its lee side. He did not know what he would want it for, and by now that was no reason to leave a thing out.
   150	
   151	Lira had not sat down. She had walked on along the wall, past the fin, to the place where the bay shut; and after he had drunk he went after her, in two stints, across shale that sighed and crept under his boots.
   152	
   153	She was standing where the bench began again, and it was not a bench any longer.
   154	
   155	"That's it," she said. "That's his ledge."
   156	
   157	It ran out from her feet along the wall of the gorge, dead level, as straight as a plank laid on trestles. He judged it six feet wide, and in places eight. For most of its length a roof of rock leaned over it. It went on for three hundred yards, and there it came to a shoulder of the mountain, and beyond that shoulder was nothing but evening air, with a brown valley under it, and in the valley a pale thread that was a road.
   158	
   159	"Three hundreds," he said. "That's all that's left of his table."
   160	
   161	"And then down the other side to a ford, and a man counts out seventy." She had her arms folded. Her right foot was set a little forward, on its whole sole, and she was looking along the ledge as he had seen her look along a painted floor at Norhold before the bell. "It's the best ground on the whole mountain. It's rock from end to end. There isn't a plate on it."
   162	
   163	"It's six feet wide."
   164	
   165	"So was the seventh, near enough, and that was a crust. This is a shelf." She glanced back, once, at the face above the bay, and lowered her voice though they were alone. "I'll run it at first light."
   166	
   167	He said nothing against it. It was her work; she had chosen it on the first day, and he had written sixty-two lines on the strength of her feet.
   168	
   169	They went back to the fire. Brom had the bacon out, and Karis was holding the long leaf to the flames to read down her last column, and up on the slope the scraping had begun to slow as the cold came on.
   170	
   171	He wrote with his back against the third box.
   172	
   173	*Seventy-seventh day. The bay at the west end of the bench. Sixty-two lines, and Karis's table says yes to every one of them, with a reason. Eight narrowings. The seventh has a hand to spare. The eighth is broken clean across, two feet, and Brom took the cart over it on his back; his wrist is dry. The tail-board did not bang. Lira wants that entered.*
   174	
   175	*In hand, forty-six. Seventy more at the ford.*
   176	
   177	*The nine: held, the whole length of it. Anchor asked at the seventh as it has never asked. Deployment since the Line: one.*
   178	
   179	*The traverse holds. The map's good. Karis says the herd moves off declarations, which means the loudest thing on this mountain is us, and we've been quiet for five days. One more day. First honest job I've ever had that didn't have a fight at the end of it. Sleep.*

