   261	The air broke round her. She felt it in her teeth.
   262	
   263	Then there was rock under both feet, and she was standing on it, twenty feet back, with her arms out and her heart going like a mill. Behind her the ledge roared down into the gorge. She had not chosen where to come down, either. Her feet had found sound stone under the roof by themselves, and stopped dead on it, as she had spent a month on a river bar teaching them to stop.
   264	
