   177	She was at the next gate on the eighty-sixth, five days after she had said.
   178	
   179	It was the hold that had given her the coat. She did there what she always did. She ate, and slept her two hours, and walked the bench; and at the end of it sat a man whose shoulder had come out of its place a week before, hauling a calf. His brothers had put it back for him, kneeling on his chest, since there was nobody else. It was in. It was not right. She spent two on it that should have been one, and she did not need to be told whose five days those were.
   180	
   181	There was a woman on that bench, too, with a breast-fed child that would not thrive, and an old herdsman who wished to be told that the pain in his side was nothing. She told the one what to do and the other the truth. Neither of them cost her a mending, and both of them had needed her on the day; and that was the part of the round that nobody at a healing house would have understood, for a house counted what its hands did, and never what its eyes had spared them.
   182	
   183	She gave that hold a night and a morning, and went on. They had put a loaf and a cold fowl in the off pannier without being asked, as they did every round, and the smith's girl had looked at the near hind hoof. It was how the coat had come to her. Nobody at any of the seven gates had ever spoken of a price, and nobody had ever let her leave with less than she came with.
   184	
   185	The route was the only thing she had ever made. She thought of that on the road, where there was time. She had not been given it. There had been no round when she crossed the Line, only a rumour in a ferry-town that the holds had no hands; and she had gone to the first gate, and mended what was there, and asked where the next fence was. It had been four holds, the first year. She had put the fifth on in her third round, and the sixth for an old woman's sake. The seventh she had added because of a child.
   186	
   187	So she knew how a stop was put on. It was not done lightly. Every stop was two days, and every two days was a day that six other gates waited; she had sat on a chock-stone with a pencil in her teeth, the night she added the seventh, and worked it through until the round came out at twenty.
   188	
   189	The child was at the next gate.
   190	
   191	She got there on the eighty-ninth evening, and she did not eat first. That was a rule broken, and she knew it, and she went straight through the yard to a house under a pear tree and put two fingers on a small wrist and two on a neck.
   192	
   193	Her lips moved to ten.
   194	
   195	The banks were where they had always been, narrow and clear, a little ragged on the left side where the breath came hard in the cold. It was running. It was no worse than it had been twenty-five days ago, and perhaps a shade better.
   196	
   197	She sat down on the floor of that house, and the child's mother put a bowl in her hands.
   198	
   199	It had been nothing. She had been five days late, and for five days she had not known, and it had been nothing, this round. She ate the whole bowl. Her hands had told her the truth as easily as they ever had; they had gone in and come out full, and she sat and looked at them in the firelight with a relief that she would not have owned to anybody.
   200	
   201	It was not her hands, then. She had half thought, on the hill out of Oxhollow, that something in them might have begun to fail.
   202	
   203	It was he.
   204	
   205	She left that hold at first light on the ninety-first, with two days of road in front of her and the mule going well. And somewhere on the first of those days, between a ford and a stand of the red trees, she found that she had been doing a sum for the better part of a morning without having set herself to it.
   206	
   207	Three ribs, broken on the seventy-eighth. Twenty-one days to knit, in anybody. By what she had felt under her hand they would be sound in him some days sooner. But she could not read him, and so she could not know it, and she had never in her life lifted a tape on a guess. It would be the ninety-ninth, then, by the rule. The long one wanted him whole and still, and it wanted the better part of a day out of her besides, so that the hundredth was the first morning on which she could run it.
   208	
   209	She would be at Thornwater on the ninety-third, and he would be fifteen days from the break. She would have her two days at that gate, and the carter's hand, which she owed, and whatever was on the bench; and on the ninety-fifth the round would take her out by the far road.
   210	
   211	The hand troubled her more than she liked. A split across the palm is a small thing on the day, and she had left it clean and wrapped, and told the man to keep it out of a rein. He would not have kept it out of a rein. He had a load for Thornwater and one pair of hands, and he had given her the news of a late crew when he might have held his tongue and had his mending. By the ninety-third that hand would be fifteen days old. She owed it to him as she owed nothing else on the round, and she meant to pay it before she sat down.
   212	
   213	So he would be whole four days after she had gone. She would be two gates on by then, and the round would not bring her back to Thornwater for twenty days more; and a crew off a board does not keep whole for twenty days. He would be on some other road, and as like as not broken in some other place.
   214	
   215	She walked a mile on that.
   216	
   217	She was not a person who wanted things. She had wanted one thing, once, which was to keep a list in the right order, and she had got it, and it had filled her days so full that there was no room left over. She had not thought that there was anything else her hands were hungry for.
   218	
   219	They were hungry for this. She had spent her life reading what people were built like, and she had been proud of never meeting a body she could not go into. Now she had met one. She did not want to mend him; there was nothing to mend that a strip of linen could not see to. She wanted to lay both hands on him, whole and still, for as long as it took, and feel exactly how it was that he could not be read.
   220	
   221	What he was she left alone. That was a question for people who kept books.
   222	
   223	On the morning of the ninety-second, on a level stretch with frost in the ruts, she did the thing she had done three times before.
   224	
   225	She took it out of the round. It was five days from the ninety-fifth to the hundredth, and she went through them one at a time, as she went along a bench. Four she could spend at Thornwater's own table without lying to anybody. The grain was moving; carters came through that yard from every hold up the valley, and a bench that filled twice in two days would fill again in four. They would make her late at six gates all the same, and she knew every one of the people she would be keeping waiting. But they were work.
   226	
   227	The fifth was not. On the hundredth she would mend nobody. She would spend a mending's worth of herself on a boy with nothing wrong with him, to learn a thing that would help no one on her route; and no hold owed her that day, and she owed it to none of them. If it was to be had, it must be added.
   228	
   229	She put a day on the route.
   230	
   231	Then she clicked her tongue to the mule, and went on at the same pace as before, on the low side, toward a gate that was still a day's walk off.
   232	
   233	---
   234	
   235	It was still the eighty-fourth day, and the light was going off the hill above Oxhollow, when Teague counted his three. He had done it before the healer's mule was out of hearing.
   236	
   237	They were behind him on the hill. The spear had not changed pace for the stop. The boy had both mules by one rein and was looking back up the road after the woman, with his mouth a little open, as he looked after everything. The bow had stood square on both legs for as long as the healer's eyes were on that hip, and now the weight had gone over to the right again.
   238	
   239	Three. Whole, and fed at noon, and one of them no better off than a week ago.
   240	
   241	They had done two holds' work in four weeks, a drain cut and a byre roofed, and there was nothing in either that would be talked of at a plank. He did not mind that. Most of what kept a crew at the top of a sheet was work of that kind, done to the day and brought home without a mark on anybody; a man who wanted his lines to be stories did not keep his people long.
   242	
   243	It was the bow's hip that he minded, and he put it first, since it was the cost.
   244	
   245	He had timed the second of his two slips to end at the upper hold on the eighty-first day. That was the day the healer's round should have brought her to its gate. He had said nothing of it to the bow; he had only found a day's more work on a byre roof that might have waited, and stayed. She had not come, and nobody at that gate could say why. On the hill she had given him no more than that she was late.
   246	
   247	He did not blame her. A healer on a round is not late for nothing, and he had never known this one's reasons to be poor ones.
   248	
   249	But it was a day he had spent and a thing he had hoped for, and he could not set it down against five names. Pike had struck a ford off his hold and given it to them; that had cost nothing, because he had chosen the pass himself. The healer's lateness was not theirs on any ground he had read.
   250	
   251	The road came round the shoulder of the hill, and Oxhollow lay under him.
   252	
   253	He read the yard through the open gate as he went by it, without turning his head more than a walking man does.
   254	
   255	There was no cart. That was the first thing, and it was the largest. He had heard on the road that they had hired the smith's, and there were three boxes standing against the gate-house wall on the bare ground, and a crew that has wheels does not stand its goods on the ground.
   256	
   257	The mild one was by the gate-post. Teague found him at once, this time, and was pleased with himself for a breath, until he saw that the man had been looking at him since the shoulder of the hill.
   258	
   259	The big one sat on the well's coping with a box-rope coiled over his knee. His left wrist had a cuff of clean linen on it, tied with a knot that Teague knew, since he had seen it on his own people. Mended, then, and under orders. The girl was on a trough. She had one boot, and the other foot was bare and strapped, crosswise, by somebody who had been shown how. It was on its whole sole. He had read those feet in an inn as the quickest thing in the room, and he would not have laid a copper on them now.
   260	
   261	The small pale one was asleep sitting up, with her head on a box and a book shut under her hand.
   262	
   263	There were four of them, and he had counted them rightly.
   264	
   265	The old keeper was on his bench by the stone house, with a tally-stick, and he gave Teague the rest in the time it took two mules to pass.
   266	
   267	"It's a no," he said. "Over the top. I've paid seventy to be told so, and I'd pay it again. They put their own cart in my gorge finding out."
   268	
   269	"All five came down?"
   270	
   271	"All five. On their feet, the most of them."
   272	
   273	Teague went on down to the water.
   274	
   275	So he had it, and there was nothing in it that a man could not have read off a yard and a keeper. They had cleared a ford with five, in a way that nobody on those roads had cleared one before, and they had walked a mountain and brought back a true answer; and they had lost a cart doing it, and come down with three of them marked.
   276	
   277	He had four things to say to that boy. He priced each of them, on the bank, as he would have priced a stage.
   278	
   279	The first was the two jobs, set side by side with the same number on each. It would cost him nothing, and it would tell the boy that he was being read, and by what. That was fair. The boy had read him across a room and not troubled to hide it.
   280	
   281	The second cost Teague something, and he had known that he would pay it since the fifty-seventh day. The ford had been good work. To say so aloud was to say that the slip had gone where it ought, and that he had no claim left on it, even in his own head. It was true, and a true thing was owed.
   282	
   283	The third would land hardest, and he turned it over longest. He did not mean it unkindly. A cart was not a carter. Nobody lay in that gorge. But a crew that went out with another man's wheels and came back without them had done a thing that the board would enter, and it was better that they heard the word for it from him, on a river bank, than read it on a wall.
   284	
   285	The fourth was the only one that he might have kept. It gave something away. It told five people at the bottom of a sheet that the name at the top expected to stand on a road beside them before the cold term was out; and it set a term on that, which was his to set, since he had three behind him who would be standing there too.
   286	
   287	He looked back, once. The spear. The boy. The bow, on the good leg.
   288	
   289	He decided to say it. A man who meant to share a contract owed the other crew the warning.
   290	
   291	The boy was sitting on the gravel at the water's edge, alone, with a book on his knee. He held one arm in a little against his left side, and under the shirt, when he breathed, something drew a line across his ribs. There was a healer's tape on him. That meant ribs; and ribs, by her rule, were three weeks under linen, counted from whichever day the mountain had done it.
   292	
   293	Teague still could not tell what he was. A Force stood one way and a Wind another, and there was no Path in the set of that boy that a man could name, and no want of one. He went down the bank.
   294	
   295	---
   296	
   297	Cael heard the mules on the gravel and shut the book on his pencil.
   298	
   299	He had known who it would be. A crew had come down the north road at a walking pace, in single file, without a word passing among them; and the man in front had a band of black leather laced on his right wrist and a hand-axe in his belt. He came to a stand a stride and a half from where Cael sat. It was the second time that day that somebody had chosen that distance.
   300	
   301	He gave no name, and he wanted none.
   302	
   303	"You cleared Thornwater's ford with five and lost a cart on the high bench with five."
   304	
   305	"Yes."
   306	
   307	It was the first word that Cael had ever said to him. There was no other to put with it. He could have told the man about a ledge that showed no crack, and a page marked *derived*, and sixty yards of fire held for eight seconds by a person who had never held a third of it. All of that was true, and all of it was his to know and nobody's to plead.
   308	
   309	"The ford was good work. The bench was a cart."
   310	
   311	There was no contempt in it. He said it as Pike said a line, level, so that it could be called wrong if it was wrong; and he waited, as Pike waited.
   312	
   313	Cael went through it for the place where he could say so. He thought of a smith in Lowmarch, and six marks owed, and a tail-board that had not banged. He thought of what a ford-keeper had counted out, and what the ford-keeper had said he was paying for.
   314	
   315	He had no argument. The man had not said that they had failed. He had said what the bench was, on a board that wrote down outcomes, and it was that.
   316	
   317	"The board's going to have you and me on the same contract inside a season. Be good enough that I'm glad."
   318	
   319	He nodded, the width of a finger, as he had nodded once from a sill. Then he walked on into the river, and his three came after him, each at the same distance from the next.
   320	
   321	They crossed without a sound. Cael watched it as he would have watched a thing well made. The one with the spear went second and grounded it at every step. The one with the bow walked a little short on one side and kept the pace regardless. A boy of sixteen or so brought the two mules through on a slack rein, and not one of the four looked down at the water. They had kept the hold on roads since before he knew there was one.
   322	
   323	He opened the book again while there was light.
   324	
   325	*Eighty-fourth day. Oxhollow, at the ford. The map is delivered: sixty-three lines, and the last one says no. Seventy marks, counted. Earned, a hundred and thirty-two. In hand, a hundred and sixteen. Six owed to the smith for a cart that is in a gorge.*
   326	
   327	*Mine: three ribs taped, twenty-one days from the seventy-eighth; the calf bound in her cloth. Lira: a leg that is new, an ankle that is taped and will not be mended. Brom: mended, and not to pull. Karis: empty, and filling. Seln: nothing.*
   328	
   329	*The nine: held, on a mountain and off it. Deployment since the Line: one.*
   330	
   331	*Teague priced us. He's right about the cart. Oryn read me three times and got nothing three times and wants a fourth. Two people out here measuring me, and neither of them has a seal. I keep waiting to mind.*
   332	
   333	He read the last of it over, and it stood.
   334	
   335	On the far bank four people and two mules were going up out of the river into the dusk, quiet, a crew's length apart. He turned his head the other way. The north road climbed the brown hill behind the hold in one long slant, and a mile up it, small and steady, there was a mule, and on the low side of the mule a woman, walking at a pace that she could keep until dark.

