   162	
   163	Cael had half risen to say something, and he sat down again. He had been on the point of telling Brom that the week was out, and that he was free of it. He saw now that it would have been like telling a man the price of something he had made himself. Nobody had kept that week but Brom. He could finish it in whatever way he pleased.
   164	
   165	---
   166	
   167	The moss came on the fourth day, and the carter stopped his mules at the edge of it without being asked.
   168	
   169	The valley had flattened and gone wet. For two hundred yards and more in front of them there was no pasture and no alder, only black water standing in sheets among yellow reed, with tussocks between as big as kneeling sheep; and across the whole of it somebody had laid a road. It was made of logs. They lay side by side, crosswise, each as thick as a man's leg and twice as long as the cart was wide, bedded on brushwood and sunk to half their depth. They ran away over the moss in a grey ribbed strip, like the backbone of something very long.
   170	
   171	"How old is that?" said Karis. She had sat up.
   172	
   173	The carter lifted one shoulder. "My father's."
   174	
   175	"And when was it last mended?"
   176	
   177	He lifted the other.
   178	
   179	Brom had gone forward onto the first of the logs. He stood on it, and on the next, and then he came to one that was darker than its neighbours and put his heel down hard, and the heel went in. He drew it out with a sucking noise. The wood under the bark was the colour of wet bread.
   180	
   181	"One in ten," he said. "Maybe one in six. They've gone soft from the middle, where you can't see it, and they'll bear a man. I'd not put a loaded axle on them." He looked back at the carter. "What did you weigh, coming this way last time?"
   182	
