   243	They made their camp at the turn of the ruts, eighty yards back from the stone, and there was no fire. Seln had chosen the place. It lay a little below the crest on the western side, so that a person lying down could not see the ring and could not be seen from it. They ate cold bread from Thornwater's oven, and the hold's blankets went round, and the mule stood with her tail to the wind.
   244	
   245	"Two and two," said Seln, "and nobody alone. I shall take the first with whoever will have it."
   246	
   247	"I will," said Brom.
   248	
   249	The mule had settled the matter of the line for herself an hour before. She had been left with her rein under a rock, and she had drawn it out and grazed her way down the old road; and when Oryn went to fetch her she was standing with her forefeet on one side of the grey stone and her hind feet on the other, pulling at the grass, as easy as a beast in its own paddock.
   250	
   251	"She doesn't know it's there," said Lira.
   252	
   253	"She's nothing in her for it to take." Oryn had the rein, and she stood looking at her animal's four small hooves with an expression that Cael could not read. "I've walked her round this place thirty times, by the ruts, because I was told to. I never once thought to ask what she'd have done if I'd let her choose."
   254	
   255	Shadow lifted then, as it lifted at every dusk. Cael felt it come up soft behind the other eight, as it had come up beside a turf fire on the fifth day and at a ford on the fifty-third, offering to take him out of the light; and he told it no. It had come at the turn of the ruts as it came anywhere. He wondered whether it would have come a stride beyond the stone, and thought that he knew, and did not go to see.
   256	
   257	He walked up to the crest once more before he lay down, since he wished to see the place with the day going off it.
   258	
   259	The sun was under the western hills. The grass had gone grey, and the eleven stones were black against the last of the sky, and all the long slope between had lost its colour and lay like ash. Only one thing in that whole country was holding the light. It was the pale level thing among the feet of the stones. He still could not have said what it was, at three hundred yards. It lay there like a pool, and the last light stood on it as light stands on water after the banks have gone dark; and it went on shining, faintly, for a long while after it should have stopped.
   260	
   261	He was not the only one who had come up to look.
   262	
   263	The healer was at the stone. She stood on the near side of it, with her boots together, like a person at the edge of a pond. She had both arms stretched out in front of her at the height of her waist, so that her two hands were over the line and the rest of her was not, and the palms were turned down.
   264	
   265	She kept them so. He did not go nearer, and he did not speak. He watched a square dark figure stand at a stone with its hands held out over nothing, as still as the uprights beyond, until the pale thing on the far slope had faded and there was no light left anywhere for her to see them by.

