+# Book 7 whole-arc read — running notes
+
+Reader: Codex, independent fresh-context whole-arc seat. Date: 2026-10-11.
+
+Scope: Book 7 manuscript chapters 1–60 in order. These are working notes for the completion report, not manuscript revisions.
+
+## Binding pre-read notes
+
+- Reader Standard: fluent thirteen-year-old; clean language, moral clarity, consequential non-lurid violence, one-pass comprehension, audio-first referents.
+- Book 7 is season-blind and uses feet/yards/paces/cords, not metric units. Generic `season` and `month` are allowed.
+- Binding owner defaults: sell the Book 6 cart and gelding during Lowmarch's first week and seal the proceeds as reserve (#23); Oryn leaves the mule at Thornwater (#24); keep the #49 protected Quiet-line wording, #50 `Six years I've wanted a face`, and #51 `Tomorrow makes three` verbatim and unglossed.
+- Book 6 seam: the shelf is the second camp beyond the Line and first fire; Seln's case came east in his satchel and rides under canvas; the Velmere letter stays at Halcenvane.
+- No proposed edit may touch a protected run.
+
+## Ordered-read notes
+
+### Movement 1 — chapters 1–7
+
+- Entry seam holds: day two on the shelf and the second camp/first fire are explicit (1:3–7); the three boxes and Seln's shut case travel east (1:36–41, 3:128–137); the book inventories Hesk and Vell's letters but no Velmere letter (1:168–183).
+- Calendar holds through day forty-nine: day nine Lowmarch (2:3), day forty-one road job (5:3), stillhound encounter day forty-three (6:219–223), six days at Thornwater and descent day forty-eight (7:3), Lowmarch return day forty-nine (7:7, 269–279).
+- Deployment holds at none. Cael suppresses every fragment on the road (5:165–183); the chapter logs say none (6:219–223; 7:273–279). Shadow's practice drifts in chapters 1 and 4 are explicitly rent/training, not field deployment (1:206–215; 4:254–265).
+- Thornwater geography is consistent: the white stone is 110 yards from the gate (6:9, 107–115, 119–129), and the stillhound keeps forty-five yards behind the party (5:189–201; 6:107–115, 129–137).
+- The stillhound's sensing mechanism remains unknown. The characters know only its station, silence, following, gravel refusal, and lack of response (6:107–115); Seln infers that it waits on an unknown signal (6:127–149).
+- Lowmarch's board and social rules are legible on one read: Pike records and speaks deeds, not ranks (2:177–201; 7:49–65); the crew enters thirteenth with eleven lines between them and Teague (7:183–193, 217–229); the cart/gelding proceeds are sealed as reserve (3:101–137), distinct from working cash (7:269–279).
+- Names stay controlled: Thornwater's holder, bowman, carter, and household remain unnamed; Teague's crew are roles only when seen (7:301–345).
+- The first major argument lands cleanly in Cael's day-forty-nine log: no instrument reads him, while the board reads what the crew did (7:269–295). It feels earned by the public correction and public ledger in the same chapter (7:27–65, 69–103), not merely asserted.
+- Repetition watch: "looked ... twice" is already a Pike tag (2:27–31, 3:3–9, 7:35–47, 157–159, 201–203); accounting diction (counted, entered, line, book, paid) is intentionally dense and may become texture-heavy across later movements. Legacy tic present once conspicuously: "Seln found that he liked him" (6:151–155).
+- Reader-standard watch only: the explanation of Pike's reputational threat is long but clear (7:69–103). No violence is lurid.
+- No finding yet requiring a line fix.
+
+### Movement 2 — chapters 8–14
+
+- Calendar and count hold: fifty-first-day departure and no deployment (8:131–169), reconnaissance on day fifty-two (8:185–301; 9:3–321), ford on day fifty-three (10:3–289; 11:405–419), then recovery through days fifty-four–sixty-nine (12:3–29, 191–243; 13:227–231; 14:163–167, 337–451). Deployment changes from none to one only after the single Wind burst (11:159–167, 405–419).
+- The stillhound discovery has an unusually sound causal chain for a young reader: Seln's seven and dry-foot observation (8:285–301; 9:125–177), the survivor's account (9:75–115), Karis's explicitly untested two-part hypothesis (9:181–225), and the planned test (9:229–317) all precede the ford proof (10:55–107, 135–169; 11:385–399). Karis calls it a finding only afterward (11:241–251, 375–399).
+- Fragment rules hold at the ford: Ember is one seed-point (10:55–93); Brom drops Iron before taking the bite (10:135–169); Wind's landing locks Cael for one count (11:159–183). Shadow is not Cael's deployment: he suppresses it (10:35–39; 11:149–159), while Seln's submerged concealment is seen only as a visually uninteresting man and never named or attributed by Oryn (10:19–25).
+- The action is frightening and exact but not lurid. The sharpest wounds are presented through function and aftermath—Lira's roll (10:257–285), Karis's palms (11:23–57), Brom's forearm (11:221–251), Cael's calf (11:209–217)—and the prose turns promptly to treatment (11:285–357) and accounting (11:361–435).
+- Body continuity holds through recovery: Lira is told the ankle will bear but never be what it was (11:297–311) and later runs with reduced/later bend (14:359–401); Cael's calf gives eleven strides before locking (12:3–7) and nine uphill by day sixty-nine (14:435–443); Brom's forearm reopens after he catches the cart (13:75–79, 133–155) and is shut-not-sound by day sixty-six (14:403–419); Karis's palms prevent grip and writing until day sixty-eight (12:109–153; 14:423–429).
+- Money holds: nine in hand before the ford (8:131–169), one mark paid for treatment after the forty is already held at Pike's box (13:19–29), forty paid into the purse makes forty-eight in hand / fifty-eight earned (14:81–105), then the four-mark well makes fifty-two (14:337–343). The sealed cart reserve remains excluded.
+- Quiet ground is still rumor, not established truth: an unnamed carter's wife gives the name and folk rule in passing (12:303–321); Karis explicitly says she cannot source the noun and that none knows what it is (12:333–357; 14:439–451).
+- The measurement argument turns rather than repeats: Cael's freedom becomes Lira's lost floor (13:159–205); Brom asks what work without a Path pays and receives an eight-mark answer (13:293–321); Seln learns that his unasked-for reports remain his (13:255–275). These are distinct costs of the same new country.
+- Strong Reader-13 aid: ford plan is restated through position, trigger, and count before execution; chapter 11's opening inventories the surviving animals and re-derives the follower behavior (11:3–17, 71–107), preventing action geography from becoming opaque.
+- Repetition watch: the exact move "looked ... once, and then ... again" recurs heavily around Pike and evaluative looks (8:35–39, 103–107; 11:321–327; 13:281–289, 315–317; 14:17–25, 457–461). "Entered" and accounting/log language are thematic, but appear in nearly every reflective scene. Hands are deliberately foregrounded to the point of saturation (8:95–115; 9:241–257; 11:23–57; 12:109–153; 14:423–429).
+- Potential clarity watch, not yet a fix: ch12's first paragraph says Cael gets eleven strides and locks "at the twelfth" (12:3–7), while his own ford log later reports the leg gives nine strides uphill (14:439–443); terrain and healing explain the difference, but frequent numeric recovery measures demand close cross-book checking.
+
+### Movement 3 — chapters 15–22
+
+- Calendar holds: the job is taken day seventy (15:1–97), cart hired/day seventy-one departure (15:157–203), shelf day seventy-three (16:229–237), herd study day seventy-five (17:205–211), days seventy-six–seventy-seven narrowings/bay (18:3–29, 171–179), collapse day seventy-eight (19:3–15, 233–245), rain day seventy-nine and rescue day eighty (20:89–165), Oxhollow day eighty-four (21:297–315; 22:323–331).
+- Deployment log remains one because it tracks Cael's fragments, not every crew Path: he refuses Anchor/Iron read on the traverse (16:131–139; 18:39–57; 21:89–101) and refuses Compression/Anchor at impact (19:141–157); nightly and end logs correctly stay at one (18:171–179; 20:375–385; 22:323–331). Lira's involuntary Wind and Karis's nine-point/eight-channel Ember road are separately explicit (19:37–57, 95–173, 257–277).
+- Fragment/Path limits hold: Iron read's range is stated as a pace and a half (16:135–139; 21:91–99); Lira's Wind takes her twenty feet and locks into a learned landing (19:37–45, 257–265); Karis empties after eight seconds/sixty yards (19:125–173; 20:41–53); Tide is hands-on and reserve-limited (20:205–263, 267–305).
+- Mountain geography and map arithmetic are trackable: 260-yard fan (16:13), 100-yard map stretches (16:157–177), 30-foot outward-pitched shelf (16:147–175), eight narrowings with the seventh 5'9" and the eighth a two-foot gap (18:29–61, 65–129), bay/fin/300-yard ledge (18:133–165), ledge lost for thirty-one paces (20:113–133; 21:47–111). Final table has 63 lines and the last says no (21:215–216; 21:333–361).
+- The high-traverse length is earned. The work accumulates genuine procedure and meaning—repeatable measurements rather than uncheckable fragment knowledge (16:131–191), Lira's speed finding a use outside a ladder (19:233–245), Brom's load authority (18:91–123), Seln's freely offered bearing reports (16:197–217; 20:113–133)—before the disaster reverses the apparent no-fight job (18:171–179; 19:33–91). It does not feel like padding despite exceeding the map's 40k guide.
+- Body continuity holds: collapse reopens Cael's calf and breaks three ribs (19:151–159; 20:27–35); Lira's tibia is set/mended while the old ankle is explicitly left knit wrong and not mended (20:205–263; 21:155–207); Brom's forearm opens a second time at the wrist, is mended, then cuffed against pulling (19:203–209; 20:271–281; 21:29–43); Karis is empty, not physically injured, and slowly regains pencil use (20:41–53; 21:211–216).
+- Oryn's boundary is clear and humane: she can read Cael's bone but not the person beyond it (20:323–369), refuses to mend what she cannot read (21:29–43), repeats the failed surface reading without naming him (22:47–87), and plans a later deep read only when he is whole (22:81–91, 127–133). She does not yet know of fragments.
+- Oryn's schedule arithmetic holds in her POV: day eighty-four meeting; expected day eighty-one missed; next gates eighty-six/eighty-nine; ninety-first departure; Thornwater due ninety-third; extra day makes the deep read possible on the hundredth (22:159–231). This is dense but unusually well anchored.
+- Money holds: fifty-two in hand before six-mark cart hire (15:3–7, 157–161), forty-six on the mountain (18:171–177), seventy paid at Oxhollow gives 116 in hand, with six still owed for the lost cart (21:345–361; 22:323–329). Reserve remains inside the saved third box (19:103–119; 20:27–37).
+- Teague's crew remains unnamed and unread as individuals beyond roles. Oryn does notice the bow's old hip without touching (22:159–173), but no name or Path is supplied. Teague's four lines to Cael are measured and exact (22:303–319); later quotation must match them word for word where protected.
+- Reader-13 watch: the shale-back mechanism is carefully distinguished as derived, not tested (17:169–221; 21:333–339). The simultaneous ledge failure plus declaration is explicitly left as two causes in an accident rather than proof (21:333–339), avoiding false certainty. Mountain peril is intense but never gore-driven.
+- Repetition watch: counting/numbering is exceptionally dense here, though mostly functional. New recurring sentence frame: a claim followed by "That was..." explanatory gloss (e.g. 15:57–71; 16:135–139; 19:37–43; 20:27–37). Hands remain an organizing image in nearly every chapter, now joined by "the low side" as Oryn's physical/moral tag (20:157–175; 21:115–131; 22:127–133, 157–173).
+
+### Movement 4 — chapters 23–29
+
+- Calendar holds: Oxhollow departure day eighty-five (23:49–53); Brom's cuff ends exactly at noon day eighty-seven (23:133–163); final road camp day eighty-nine (23:225–265); Lowmarch day ninety and job taken day ninety-one (25:213–225); Thornwater day ninety-three (26:155–215); rise reached late day ninety-five (27:157–183); ring survey/trials day ninety-six at noon (28:67–105; 29:3–19).
+- Money/obligations hold: 116 at Oxhollow, two-mark boots => 114 (23:71–107); six paid to smith => 108 (24:233–251); sixty-yard/four-strand rope remains a non-monetized debt to ferryman (24:3–19); the hundred is lodged but not yet earned (25:223–247). The sealed reserve is shown and deliberately left at Thornwater (27:13–19).
+- Board position holds: mountain raises them to tenth, while Teague remains nine lines above (25:3–49). Two holds ask for the crew by five names and wait a fortnight, then pass to Teague (24:37–59; 25:251–257). No member of Teague's crew is named.
+- Quiet ground's knowledge boundary develops in the right order: rumor/carrier map first; Pike names Fallow Ring and explains only the inherited road rule (24:147–179); Karis triangulates location/contract/map without claiming mechanism (24:183–229); ring boundary is found empirically by five practitioners on one stride (27:181–229). “Quieting” has not been named.
+- The registry connection is evidence, not decoded truth: cream form style (25:159–179), westward query (26:59–105), and registry-stamped iron 31 at the same measured boundary (28:109–145, 217–237). Cael explicitly waits for the third point before drawing a line (26:99–105; 28:131–139). The stake number is not decoded.
+- Geography holds and is readable: road bends round; ring is about 300 yards from crest (27:157–171); boundary is eighty yards from the road turn (27:181–219); circuit is sixty-two cords plus twenty strides / 1,374 yards and a piece, with ten cords to center (28:189–211); registry stake lies a boot-width from the boundary on a line toward center (28:217–237). Eleven broken uprights enclose the pale floor (27:165–167; 28:159–165).
+- Oryn's agency remains hers. The crew does not ask her to leave her route (25:193–203; 26:189–215); she independently adds Cael as a stop and chooses the Ring because she refuses inherited claims about her own hands (26:193–211). Her trial establishes that Tide itself, not Brom's unreadability, is absent inside (29:185–235).
+- Cael's deception is clear and not softened: he secretly tests Iron read and Wind inside (29:243–287), then gives Karis a technically true but materially incomplete answer and watches her record the false implication (29:291–309). The narration calls it “not the truth” and “the first thing of that kind” kept from the crew (29:305–309). This sets an ethical debt before the later taking.
+- Deployment log remains one through ch29 (23:257–265; 25:213–219; 27:233–239). These private Ring trials are not counted as field deployments per the ledger's binding definition.
+- The Ring trials make the book's central argument concrete without making “no Path” equal “no person”: Brom's box remains a hundredweight (29:75–87); Seln's craft remains effective without Shadow (29:89–115); Lira discovers trained legs after Wind gives only a step (29:119–181); Oryn retains ordinary medical hands but no Tide (29:185–237).
+- Repetition watch: chapters 24–25 repeat the hidden-paper discovery almost beat for beat from Cael and Teague POVs (stone, dusty corner, second reading, assessment). This is intentional comparative characterization, but the same-content replay is long (24:123–229; 25:53–107) and should be checked in source-closeness and pacing. Pike's double-look tag continues (24:23–35; 25:69–77; 26:3–13).

