   174	
   175	```
   176	FRAGMENT ACQUIRED
   177	[unnamed] — Tide-adjacent. Duration: sustained. Integration: partial.
   178	Tier equivalent: Iron.
   179	Note: current-perception component; architecture-reading component. Self and contact.
   180	Contact range.
   181	Acquisition: directed. Engagement: clinical — non-hostile.
   182	```
   183	
   184	Ten.
   185	
   186	He read it through, from the top, with her hand still on him.
   187	
   188	It said *unnamed*, as it always said; and he knew the name, and how she laced a boot. *Iron*: it was as light as the one before it, which a friend had given him on sprung oak. *Current-perception* was the going-along. *Architecture-reading* was the lamp in the rooms. *Self and contact*, and under it, *Contact range*: it did nothing whatever at the length of an arm. He had nine things that could each be used on an opponent, and here was a tenth that was of no more use in a fight than a spoon.
   189	
   190	He lay and looked at that. He had wanted it with his whole heart in a loft, and had told four people why: that he might look. It sat under his breastbone now, as real as the other nine, and he did not try it. He did not know how. And he had a healer's hand on him still, and a debt to pay before he had any right to learn.
   191	
   192	He counted what was new in it, since he could not have helped counting.
   193	
   194	It was the first that had ever come to him lying down. It had come out of nobody's blow, in a room with a basin in it, from two hands that were trying to learn what he was made of. That was one.
   195	
   196	It was the first whose last line had a healer's word in it. He had seen *non-hostile* once before, at the end of a bout with a friend, and had not known what to do with it. He had never seen *clinical*. Whatever kept those lines had looked at what she did and had found the right name for it. That was two.
   197	
   198	There was a third. He knew what it was. It was already written, on a leaf, in a loft across the yard, in his own hand, with last night's date at the head of it; and he did not set it in the row with the others.
   199	
   200	---
   201	
   202	Her hands had gone still.
   203	
   204	They had not lifted. But the slow going-along had stopped, somewhere down by his knees, and it did not set out again. He heard her breath catch and hold.
   205	
   206	He looked up at her. He had not done that since she shut her eyes. Her face hung over him, upside down, grey, with the sweat standing on it and the cropped hair dark; and the crease had come between her brows, the one that she wore over a sum that had come out oddly.
   207	
   208	Her eyes opened.
   209	
   210	She was looking straight down into his. He had seen those eyes go over a yard of people in the time that it takes to count five. They were not going over anything now.
   211	
   212	"Something just changed," she said. "In you. While I was in there."
   213	
   214	"Yes."
   215	
   216	He had told her not one true thing about himself since a hollow of shale, beyond what she had asked; and he gave her that word as he would have handed her the first coin of a debt.
   217	
   218	She did not take her hands away. He felt the right one move on his breastbone, by the width of a finger, toward the place where a cup had filled. It stopped there. She could not read him. She had said so five times. But she had been walking the length of something for twenty minutes, and she knew its gait by now as he knew hers; and it was not going as it had gone.
   219	
   220	He watched her do what she did at a bench. She did not guess. She went over what her hands had found, strictly, and came to the one thing that a healer must ask first.
   221	
   222	"Did I do that?"
   223	
   224	There was no fright in it. It was the question that she would have put if a patient had gone white under her on the ash at Oxhollow.
   225	
   226	He could have let her think so. He saw it open in front of him, as easy as a gate: it would have been a healer's hands doing something that they had never done before, to a boy that they could not read, and she would have carried it north with her and turned it over at every hold for a year.
   227	
   228	"No. I did."
   229	
   230	The crease deepened. She waited. She was a person who knew how to let somebody come to the end of his own load.
   231	
   232	He had a hundred days of road behind him in which he had been asked what he was by nobody. He had thought, on a shelf above a valley, that this was freedom, and had written it down. He lay now under the hands of the one person past the Line who had tried to find out without a seal, and who had just paid for the trying with something that he did not yet know the size of; and he had a leaf in a loft that said what he owed.
   233	
   234	"I need to tell you what I am."
   235	
   236	He had not known that it would be so hard to say. It was only eight words.
   237	
   238	Oryn looked at him for as long as it takes to draw one breath, and he could not tell what was in her face. It was not fear. It had never been that. Then the corner of her mouth went down, a very little, as it went when a patient on her table began to sit up before she had done.
   239	
   240	"Then lie still while you do it. You've been still for twenty minutes. I want to see if you can manage twenty-one."
   241	
   242	Her hands stayed where they were.

