     1	# Chapter 38 — What She Was Told
     2	
     3	He began at a brass plate, since everything else stood on it.
     4	
     5	"I was fourteen. It was a district office on a cold morning, with a chalk circle on the boards for me to stand in. Something woke behind my breastbone, as it wakes in everybody on that day: a small light at the edge of my eye. You'd call it an Arbiter." He kept his voice low and his breath slow, as she had taught it to go. "It looked at me for eleven seconds, and I counted them. There's a plate let into the desk in those offices, which lights with the name of your Path. For eleven seconds it stayed dark."
     6	
     7	Her hands lay where they had lain for twenty minutes, the left under his neck and the right on the bone, over the very place that he was speaking of.
     8	
     9	"Then it lit, late, with one word on it, and the word wasn't a Path. Karis found out what it is. It's what the form writes when it can't be filled in, and it stands where an older name used to be written, before somebody took the older name away. After that the light went out. It has been dark ever since, on a floor or a road or at your stone. When the five of you crossed that line, something stopped in each of you; and nothing changed in me, since nothing was lit to begin with."
    10	
    11	"Did it hurt? When it went out."
    12	
    13	"No. It was like a wick being pinched."
    14	
    15	He gave her the rest as the five of them had settled it in a reading room above Ostrand, a piece at a time, with nothing in it that Karis could not have sworn to. He had never said it to anybody lying down. It was easier than he had feared and a good deal worse than he had hoped, since her palm rose and fell with his breath and she could feel every place where he wished to hurry.
    16	
    17	"What came after is the part that concerns you. When somebody with a Path uses it on me, or against me, for long enough, and means it, and there's something that matters to both of us in how it comes out, then a piece of what they do stays in me afterward. They still have all of theirs; I've looked, every time. But I have a piece as well. It's smaller than theirs, and it costs me to carry, and it answers when I ask."
    18	
    19	"How many?"
    20	
    21	"Nine, when I lay down on this table."
    22	
