   271	So there had been two people in that room, at the least: there was a hand that wrote, and a voice that knew the ground.
   272	
   273	He had spent fifteen years as the first of those, and he knew what it was to write a true line at another person's bidding without being told what the line was for. He had written sixty-three subjects into bundles in that way, each of them a person with a face that he could have drawn; and he had sent every bundle up a stair to people whom he had never once seen.
   274	
   275	That was the part of it that he had not been able to make four friends understand, and tonight he had not tried.
   276	
   277	He let himself go back to the corridor, which he allowed seldom. It was distempered green to the height of a man's shoulder, in a district seat three postings and twelve years behind him, and he had stopped dead in the middle of its floor with an armful of somebody's travel endorsements. A clerk had put his head out of a side door to ask whether he was unwell. He had not been unwell. He had understood, in the space between one step and the next, what a certain run of ledgers was that he had copied the year before as dull upkeep; and whose question it had been all along.
   278	
   279	After that there had been a press, in a records room on a top floor, and five weeks, and a case.
   280	
   281	Twelve years. He had kept it by never once describing it. He had carried it from posting to posting in the bottom of a satchel, under his shirts, and written *nothing to report* every week over the top of it; and in all that time he had never learned the name of one person who had made what was inside.
   282	
   283	He knew their paper: how they folded it, and where they filed it, a little above where it should have sat, as only somebody could who was able to reach. He had their handwriting by heart, and it had not one face in it.
   284	
   285	He had been taught four grades of pressure, in their order. There was the record, and then the instrument, and then the knock, which was paper that nobody would own. Last came the hand, which was a road with no paper at all. He had believed for fifteen years that the hand was the worst of them.
   286	
   287	Then four people had come home to a hill from a chalk cut on the Ostrand road, and had told him what the hand looked like when it came: people with staves, who did their work without a word, and broke off in four heartbeats, and left nothing behind them but footmarks. He had told them that what stood in that road was a glove.
   288	
   289	A glove had something inside it. He had thought so then, and had kept it to himself, since it was not a finding.
   290	
   291	Now there was a fifth grade, which nobody had taught him, and he had held it in his two hands tonight. It was a lure, and a lure was a different kind of thing from the other four. The record could be kept by nobody in particular, and the instrument read by any clerk; the knock came through a door and the hand out of a ditch. But a lure that is taken must be met. Somebody had to be there.
   292	
   293	*People have faces.*
   294	
   295	He had said it at a fire, to four people, in his own voice, and he was a little astonished at himself still. He went over it now as he would have gone over a return before it was sealed, to see whether it was true or only well put.
   296	
   297	---
   298	
   299	It was not revenge, though he looked for that first, since it was the commonest thing. He turned out every pocket of it, and found that he did not want anybody on that rim hurt, and had no wish to see a hood pulled back by force. He had spent too long being the quiet man at the edge of a room to suppose that the people who would wait at the foot of that ridge were anything but quiet men at the edge of somebody else's room, sent by a stair that they had never climbed.
   300	
   301	What he wanted was smaller than that, and he thought that it was worse.
   302	
   303	He wanted to know that it was done by persons.
   304	
   305	For twelve years the thing in the case had been like weather to him. It had fallen on forty children, and on a good many more before them, as rain falls on a field; and a man does not go looking for the face of rain. He files what it did; and Seln had filed it, and had been very good at that, and it had let him sleep.
   306	
   307	But a boy had come up a hill with a word on his file where a tier should have been, and had looked at every instrument that was turned on him as though somebody had built it. That had been the end of the weather. If a thing was built, then there was a builder, with a walk, and a way of holding a pen, and a tiredness at the end of the day. Seln could no longer do without seeing one: he wished to stand on the same ground as a single one of them, in daylight, and learn what a person looked like who did this for his living.
   308	
   309	It was a clerk's want, since a file wanted a name at its head; he did not think that it was a noble one, and he entered it as it stood.
   310	
   311	Then he audited his vote, since he owed that.
   312	
   313	He had been asked what he would do, and he had answered for himself, and he had said that it should count for one hand. All of that was correct. It was also true that four people had heard him want something for the first time in two years, and that three of them were of an age to be moved by it. He had seen the researcher shut her mouth on a question. He had seen the big one look at him as he looked at a load that had shifted.
   314	
   315	Had he put his thumb on the scale? He thought not. The boy had ruled on his own ground, and the boy's reason was better than his: it was about what the paper did not know, and it would have stood if Seln had never spoken.
   316	
   317	He was not certain. He wrote that down too, in the place where he kept such things, which was nowhere.
   318	
   319	There was a way to be certain, and he had been looking at it since the fire was banked.
   320	
   321	The paper followed the case. He had said so himself. If the case were to go south tonight, by itself, on one man's chest, then by first light there would be nothing at this fire that anybody in the west had priced. Five people would wake to a walk at fifty and a fence at forty.
   322	
   323	He knew the south road; he had walked the first hours of it alone in their second week here, as he had walked every road out of this town. He could be on the ridge before the stars turned, and he would have his face, very likely; and an arm two weeks old would never come within a mile of that floor.
   324	
   325	His hand was on the strap, and he let it stay there, and looked at the thing squarely.
   326	
   327	He had lost an argument on a shelf above a valley with no name, on the second night past the Line. He had proposed that the watches should be his to arrange as he judged best, since he had fifteen years of judging them; and a girl with a ruled table had told him that the rota was not set aside for anybody's judgment, his own included. He had been angry for an hour and grateful ever since. A man who has decided things alone for fifteen years does not stop because he has resolved to; he stops when four people will not let him.
   328	
   329	And there was the other reason, which he had given a boy in a loft. He had told him that he would sooner see a healer warned of what might be done to her than be used as the reason for keeping her ignorant. If that was good for a healer, it was good for a crew. He could not carry the danger off in the night and leave them to find a cold place under a tree. That was the service's way, and it had been his own, every week, for fifteen years: to know what was best for a subject, and arrange it, and say nothing.
   330	
   331	He had resigned from that. He had merely never filed it.
   332	
   333	Four hands had gone up at that fire, and one had stayed down, and had asked to be entered, and was going regardless. He was not entitled to take a decision out of five people's hands by removing its object, however kindly he meant it.
   334	
   335	He took his own hand off the strap.
   336	
   337	The case lay where it had lain, against his breastbone, with its lock toward him. He knew what was in it to the leaf. He had not opened it since a reading room above Ostrand, and he did not open it now; there was nothing in it that would tell him who was waiting at the foot of a ridge, and there was one leaf in it that was not a copy.
   338	
   339	The stars had turned as far as they turned in one watch, and he got up, without a sound, and went round the four sleepers, and the boxes, and the mule, as he had gone round every camp of theirs since the shelf. Then he bent and touched the girl's right shoulder with two fingers. He chose that shoulder with care.
   340	
   341	She woke at once, as she did everything.
   342	
   343	"Anything?" said Lira.
   344	
   345	"Nothing," said Seln.
   346	
   347	It was the report that he gave at the end of every watch. She took the blanket that he held out, and put her back against the ash, and he lay down where she had been lying, which was still warm.

