+
+- Calendar holds: Lowmarch decision night day 112 (46:187–195, 259–263); Millrace and Stair arrival day 113 (47:3–25, 121–153; 48:127–171, 273–281); trap day 114 (49:3–19; 51:193–217); survey day 115 (52:3–21, 355–359); travel days 116–118 and Hall arrival day 118 (53:59–103).
+- Stair geometry is consistent: the Quieting edge is forty paces inside the carter ring; the floor is 220 yards from the line stone (49:3–5). The floor is about thirty yards across; the stair is forty steps; its room has five courses per wall and a roof only a hand above Brom (49:159–193; 52:61–101). The faint/clear/absent Anchor bands are exact at ten/twelve/fourteen feet (50:261–267).
+- The trap's cause-and-effect is readable in one pass: a bolt draws Oryn five yards inside, three anchored courses close behind her, six observers maintain a rim, and cold/Brom's untreated wound are meant to force surrender (49:21–83, 87–121; 50:105–123). The attack is frightening and exact, not lurid; the bolt wound is described anatomically only to explain why it stays in until Tide returns (49:237–273).
+- Shadow's second and final use occurs from the fourth stair step to the old bar, entirely outside hostile lines of sight; Lira sees only its disappearance from below and Seln deliberately turns away (50:173–181, 213–255; 51:193–201). Oryn is underground and never learns its source. The usage is not attributable to a watcher (51:165–173).
+- Fragment rules hold: Wind locks one count (51:21–33); Storm's one lane bills ten counts (51:37–49, 207–213); Compression takes the crossbow blow at contact (51:51–59); Ember is one point (51:73–83); Anchor unbinds at touch (50:281–295); Tide requires the person's consent and physical contact (47:97–123; 48:11–53, 73–105). Deployment advances from thirteen to fourteen with the consensual Tide read (48:273–281), then nine Stair uses make twenty-three (51:193–217).
+- Consent reaches its necessary test at Millrace: Cael asks to read the boy and accepts Oryn's no immediately (47:97–129); later he asks Oryn a different question, receives her explicit yes, follows surface/long limits, and exits at her word (48:7–27, 29–53, 73–105). The prose explicitly identifies body ownership—the boy's leave was not Oryn's to give; her wrist is hers (48:17–23). There is no forgiveness language attached to the yes.
+- The route/crew distinction also holds: Oryn refuses membership, chooses to put them on her route, and sets the terms herself (47:145–225). Her later continuation through Brom's week and the next edge is her own counted decision, not a request coerced from her (53:37–55).
+- Bodies hold: Brom's bolt crosses the right shoulder, misses lung/major vessel, remains as a plug until Tide returns, then is drawn and mended for an hour (49:237–273; 51:125–153). Lira's ankle gives on step twenty and the forearm reopens at two newest points; the arm is rebound and mended, still barred from use (49:281–301; 51:143–153). Cael's right forearm takes the crossbow strike through Compression and his left palm is burned by Ember (51:47–83, 157–163). Oryn empties three times at Millrace, then spends two more mendings after the Stair (47:43–85; 51:125–153).
+- Names remain controlled: the six on the rim are never named or unmasked; Teague's three remain only bow/spear/boy, and Teague never asks for Cael's mechanism or receives a fragment inventory (51:225–255, 259–383). “The Bronze from Ardenmere” does not arise in a spoken list in this movement.
+- The Stair adds to the Ring. The Ring established identical suppression and Cael's exception; the Stair weaponizes those facts, proves the registry has resurveyed a known site, exposes the lure as a pre-restriction inventory, and forces the exception to be used under secrecy constraints (46:3–47; 48:175–221; 49:21–121; 50:143–181). It is not merely a second demonstration.
+- The Hall also begins by adding a third site on a measured bearing, not simply repeating a boundary: the survey ledger ends “third site confirmed on bearing” (52:177–239); the mapped line reaches the drowned hall within the admitted error of a map made in days (53:3–35, 95–119); site 33 lacks the later resurvey bar/mark (53:127–157); the submerged organism responds to staying weight, creating a new physical rule (53:191–243, 247–339).
+- The protected reserved truths hold: the stroke-and-bar mark is copied but not decoded (48:205–217; 52:139–159, 187–203); the surveyor's four-sign index is not identified (52:177–203); the Stair case is recovered but Seln's locked case remains shut. Only the authorized maker sentence is made: the floor carving knows the circle's size (52:291–311).
+- Movement 7's extra length is earned. It contains the contract's moral debate, the first refused request and first consented outward read, two-day Stair siege/resolution, Teague's independent arrival, the survey, the bearing reveal, three days of travel, and the third site's rule test. Each stage changes the available knowledge or an ethical rule (46:51–185; 47:97–225; 49:21–121; 50:143–209; 51:225–255; 52:177–311; 53:191–339). It does not read padded, though the full Teague flashback after the Stair climax delays release (51:259–383).
+- Repetition watch: the book's “counting” texture peaks here—distances, observers, bolts, consent, mendings, deployment, and route days all use the same diction. “He had never...” constructions cluster in the decision and trap aftermath (46:111–123, 153–161, 273–285; 47:115–123; 51:179–187). Hands are both subject and stage business in nearly every major exchange.
