editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:161:"Then you may have my bread," said Seln, "all of it, with my compliments," and sat down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:353:"She'll fall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:355:"She may. She'll get up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:83:"No." She let them fall. "Not the colour, and not the grain; it has no grain at all. I have a piece of every sort of dressed stone between Denvash and the Line in this box, with a date to each, and not one family of them comes near it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:155:He let Iron Skin go as a man lets a coat fall from his shoulders in a doorway, all at once, and stood in what was underneath. The grey went out of his neck and his hands, and out of the arm with the jaws shut on it, between one beat of his heart and the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:139:"That bone's a month old today. I said a month, on the mountain. It may run."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:153:He had that sum by heart, from the ninety-fifth day to the morning after a fortnight. Sixteen and one made seventeen, somewhere on a road that he had never seen; and she did not say where the one would fall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:171:"So that's two boxes on two backs, and one left over, and a case that the quiet one won't put down." She counted it on her fingers. "And a girl who's to fall on her face sooner than put a hand out, on a road that's ice in the ruts. Who steadies her, when the pair of you have a hundredweight apiece?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:67:*Not a rope, not a knife, not a fall, not a friend.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:161:"You could. And if she puts a foot in a rut, you come off, and you come off onto that." Oryn had looked at the sling. "A fall off a mule's a fall. You walk."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:199:*You were told. I shall fall more carefully.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:211:The boy was on the mule's other side, at the off shoulder. He was breathing to her beat, as he had learned to on a road while she was not looking, and every so often he stood still for the space of a breath with his weight on the right leg. She had counted his strides between. It was eleven when they left the stone. By the spring, where they halted for the night, it was nearer forty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:219:They had a fire that night, at the spring, and Seln made the bread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:267:Cael noticed it as a person notices that a noise has ceased. He had been walking at the mule's shoulder since first light, across the last of the pale grass, and he reached for the number of his strides and found that he had none; he had not stood still since the spring. He tried the calf on purpose, a long step and then a quick one. It was sore, and it was a leg.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:143:Cael had. Nobody could have missed it. Two-thirds of the way along the bay a blade of pale stone stood up out of the shale, quite alone, halfway between the foot of the face and the edge. It lay square across the fall of the ground, like a wall across a field, and it was as long as a barn and no thicker than a garden wall. At either end it was no higher than a man's waist, and in the middle it was twice his height, and the evening sun lay along its upper side and left the lower black.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:221:"Four lines. Nobody lost, nobody hurt, nobody knows you." Pike held up his one hand and folded the fingers down as he went. "A boat. A fence. A wagon out of a ditch. A mile behind a mule. I'd hire you tomorrow, myself, for any of the four. But a man comes to me from a hold with something that's eating his stock, and asks who he ought to have. I can't give him a crew that's never yet had anything go wrong." He let the hand fall. "It's a good book. It's a short one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:115:She was as wet down the front as a person can be who has lain for an hour on her stomach on a stone with a spring slopping over it. Cael saw her shiver, from the near fire, and saw that she did not know she was doing it; she had her notebook open and three clean leaves before her, and her pencil had already begun.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:191:He had stood on a wall that morning and looked down at a spring. The water there had come up against a fallen slab and piled, and curled back behind it in a slow eddy, as water does when it meets a thing lying in its road. He looked for the piling now, and there was none. Not one of the ten came up against that place. Each of them began to lean a long way short of it, and came back to its line a long way past; they went by it as a river goes round a stone that has lain in its bed for as long as there has been a river, without a ripple, along a way that was worn smooth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:9:They were the whole of the forenoon about that perimeter, with the marsh on their right and the frost going grey on the turf. Not once in that round did the water stand a yard inside the line, or a reed a yard beyond it. A spring had filled that basin for longer than anybody could say, and it had filled it to a line that five people could feel in their heads and no farther. Cael could not have said what water knew about such an edge, and not one of them tried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:173:So he did the other thing, which anybody may do. He asked him a question, in words, that he could answer or not as he chose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:97:It stood on the rolled edge a yard from Brom's right hip, with its head no higher than his belt, and it looked him over as a man looks over a cart that he may buy. Cael had it inside the read from the instant its forefeet touched the lip, and he felt the weight of it come forward, slowly, off the haunches and onto the forelegs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:109:"It can't get its legs under it. Keep close to the edge. If you give it a yard of ground it'll have its spring."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:145:They were at the south. Fifteen yards on along the kerb two grey shapes were standing on the grass, shoulder to shoulder, with their heads level. They had gone ahead on the white and dropped off the rim where there was nobody under it with a pole, and they stood now on turf, where a hound has all the spring that it was born with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:261:It was the lip a third time. A hand's height had spoiled the spring of two hounds, and now it gave a man who had never shot a bow something to rest one on. Brom went back over it and knelt on the turf, box and all, and set the stock on the rolled edge and his cheek against the stock. There were four strides and a foot of white between the iron head of that bolt and the eye that Cael was looking into, and one foot more between the eye and Cael's own face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:315:"That stays above her heart." She took Lira's left wrist and laid it against Lira's own right collarbone, and shut Lira's right hand over it. "You hold that there. You don't let it down to balance, and you don't put it out if you fall. If you fall, you fall on your face."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:297:"Quite. If water masks it, then there is one of us who may use what he has for the whole of the evening, provided that he does so up to his chin." Seln's mild face showed nothing at all. "I had meant to propose the pool beside the tail. I have not lifted my Path since a fire at a crossroads, and I find that I should rather like to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:251:"So I cannot tell you how it was made, since I have nothing to compare it to. But a thing may be dated without knowing how it was made, if it has stood in the weather, and that has." She took up the cup of wands. "I wrote down which way every stone faced. I did it from habit, and it is the best thing that I did on that floor."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:93:"A mend that deep holds nothing for a season. Nothing. That arm is not to hold. It's not to strike. It's not to catch. Not a rope, not a knife, not a fall, not a friend. Not until I say. And I'll say at first snow, near enough, if it's let alone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:103:Thirty yards off, Brom let the tail-board fall. It banged, and nobody was there to stop it. He dragged out the left-hand box and got it onto his shoulder, and the middle one after it onto the other. Then he turned with a hundredweight on either side of his head and came back over the cracking shale at a trot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:189:He did not hear her land. He heard the slide going over after her, tons of it, in a long hissing fall; and after that, at intervals, three or four heavier sounds, dull and separate, that were not stone and not a cart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:21:She had seen it. It had been a hand below her chin for a quarter of an hour, on a wall above a spring: the line that stood by itself, with its small divisions, as it stood between the courses under the Stair. She had counted those divisions there against her short cord, twice, and they had given her the radius of the circle. Here she had had a pencil in one hand and her own weight on the other, and no cord at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:61:Then the middles. She had stood on all three. One was the centre of a white floor with eleven stones about it, and one was a square hole with a stair in it, and one was a spring. She made a dot in each ring with the point of her pencil, as small as she could make it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:103:"The man with the book wrote *on bearing*," said Seln. He had not taken the ledger out. "He drew through two, as she said in that room, and he had a third confirmed along it. Hers lie along the same three. I think that we may call it one line, and his."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:11:"May I read you?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:205:She took up the cheek-strap, and let it fall again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:23:"I put that to him also. He said that the salt crews go under them twice a year on the cold road, and walk, and are let alone. The animals are as mild as cattle. A man may pass beneath a herd all day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:33:Cael looked at Karis. She had the notebook out at last; it had come out of her coat the moment the bar dropped, as though it had been on a spring. She wrote one word at the head of a clean leaf, and looked at it, and drew a single line beneath it. Then she looked up at the woman with the lantern, and he saw the second question arrive in her face, whole, with its grammar. It was the question of what a thing like that could be waiting for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:111:"It stops short," she said. She was sitting on the top box with the leaf on her knee. "Short of what? I can think of four answers, and I've no way of choosing among them. It may stop short of the fence, or of the people behind the fence. It may be that something lies under the ground at that distance and nowhere else. Or a hundred and ten is simply as near as it ever comes to anything, and the stone is an accident."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:133:He knew what he was looking at, and it was not anything that he knew about animals, of which he knew very little. It was his own trade. A man set to follow another is given his distance. He is told to keep it, and neither close nor fall back; and it is chosen so that the ordinary noise of the person in front will cover him, and so that one particular thing, if it should happen, will reach him across the gap. He does not act until it does, and he may walk behind a subject for a year and never be given leave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:25:It was a narrow green place, a good deal greener than anything on the ridge. The water came down it fast and brown over a weir of stakes and stone, and above the weir a channel had been cut away along the bank to a tall grey building with a wheel on its side as high as a barn door. The wheel went round steadily in a white fall of water. There was a fence of split posts about the mill and a dozen roofs, and below the fence a ford, with a flat stone on each bank for the wheels to aim at. Smoke rose straight up from four chimneys.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:105:"I've read one thing with what I had from you, and it was myself. I found no bank in it anywhere, as you found none. I don't know what a bank is like under the hand. I've only your word that there is such a thing." He kept his own hands at his sides. "When you take the boy's wrist, may I set my hand on it too? I want to learn what reaches you through yours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:131:"It is an observation. You may put my initial against it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:167:"I know where you had it. I said it to a loft, with my hand on a ladder." She rubbed her palms together slowly. "It's what I say at every gate when I leave it. I don't ask them whether I may go. I tell them where I'll be, and then it's theirs to carry, and they can't say afterward that they weren't told. I never had it said to me before. I don't care for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:277:"Don't pass," said Lira. She had sat on a landing wall for a week learning carters, and she said it as a rule. "You don't go by a cart on a road. You fall in behind, and you wait until somebody speaks to you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:7:"There's the store behind the chimney," he said. "It's warm; it has the back of that fire for a wall. I keep meal in it." He looked at the three boxes, which Brom and Seln had brought in out of the snow. "Six, for the cold term. And you may lie at this end of the hearth for the standing-money, the five of you, as long as there's paper in my store."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:131:It was a high bare way between two hills, with nothing on it but frost and a few sheep that belonged to nobody, and she slept each night with her back against the jenny's belly and her feet in her pannier. On the fourth afternoon the ground began to fall. There was a valley under her with a pale road in it, going east and west, and she knew that road; she had gone round a ring of stones by it, thirty times, with a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:171:"And the sum. It is written in words, and then again, in a bracket. A person is taught to do that who has been warned that a figure may be altered and a word may not. I was so taught, at a ruled desk, when I was not a great deal older than he is." The mild eyes came up. "The place is given as a description. A day, and a rise, and a road that bends. Every carter in that meadow has a name for it, and our friend at the plank used the name at once. Whoever wrote this either did not know it or was not permitted to put it down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:119:"I have thought about that too," said Seln. "I could walk up those steps now, with this held over my head. They would take it. I do not think that the fence would come down afterward. A paper that sends six people to a hole in the ground is not concerned with what becomes of anybody who could say how the thing was collected." He paused. "I may be wrong. I have been inside that service, and I do not think that I am."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:181:"After, he is to do everything that he has forbidden himself all day, as loudly as he can, on the far side of that stone. Their whole plan is that nobody on this ground has a Path. The instant that one of us stands outside the fence with something in his hands, and the fence is down behind him, there is no plan. Five more of us may walk out and be what we are." Seln's head turned toward Cael. "They were sent for a case. They were not sent to fight in the dark for it. I know what my own orders would have been, and so do they."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:235:He took the southern of its two ruts, since that was the side that the newer bar stood on. It was a trough a foot deep and as wide as a wheel, with turf grown down into it, and it ran straight up the fall of the ground to a stone that he could not yet see. He had a hundred and ninety yards of it to walk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:141:It was there. His boot came down on the frozen earth of the yard as it had come down all morning. But he felt it come down half a beat after it had done so, as a man hears an axe from across a valley after he has watched it fall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:243:On the platform by the western gate the bowman had turned round. It was the one hour of the day when nothing had ever come up that valley, and he seemed to keep it for looking inward, at the hold. He had watched a boy fall about a yard eleven times, and get it right, and he stood with his bow at the length of his arm and gave no sign that it was any business of his. Then his eyes went to the girl on the coping, who had seen a fault from thirty feet with one arm tied, and they stayed there a good deal longer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:293:He carried eleven sacks more before the light went, on feet that told him of each rung a little after he had climbed it, and he did not fall off the ladder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:61:"If she slips with me on the inside, she goes that way with nothing to stop her. This way she's got something to lean on." Oryn clicked her tongue. "She's a mule, and she'd sooner lean than fall. Most things would."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:121:The second item was the side she kept. On every slant she was below the mule. When the track turned, and the fall of the hill went over to the other hand, she passed behind the animal's tail without breaking step and came up on the low side again; and she did it at every turn of that hillside without once seeming to consider which side was the low one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:281:She let the hand fall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:93:"I shall not be a reason. You shut that thing for my sake, and you have kept it shut at a cost that I watched you pay in a river. But I have had four nights in which to consider who was looking in a lane, and I find that I should sooner have her told what may be done to her than be used to keep her ignorant. Strike the second. It is not available to you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:101:"That there's nothing I could put in front of her which she could weigh. I'd be telling her a thing with no name that she has ever heard: that I take pieces of people when they come at me hard enough and mean it, and that there may be room in me for a tenth. What does a person say to that, at night, through a door?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:115:"If I tell her, she may say no. She's a person who says no; I heard her say it to a girl on a mule. And then there is no morning. She goes north in a day or two, and I never lie on that table, and the one time that it could have been tried is gone." He made himself finish. "I want it, and that's under all three. I have been a thing that instruments couldn't read since a brass plate went dark over me, and she's the first that ever tried it with her hands and told me so to my face. On that hill I was the only thing that worked, and I can't tell any of you why. If I had a piece of what she reads with, I might be able to look."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:127:The sound of his own feet came back a beat late, like an echo off a wall. His hands had gone as cold as if he had held them in a spring. And the dark-backed one, which had heard nothing come and seen nothing come, shifted its grip to finish.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:291:Cael turned as he fell. The rangy one was in the air over the stone, at the top of its leap, with its forelegs stretched and the flame's light on its teeth, and it was six feet from the nose of the bow. He did not see the bolt. He saw the animal fold in the middle of its spring, and come down on the western side of the stone like a thing dropped off a cart, and lie.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:305:He saw them only as shapes against the fire, and only for as long as it burned. They had been strung out down two hundred yards of hill behind a light, running to a declaration, and the declaration had gone over a stone and gone out; for he had let the flame die and the edge fall silent when the string slapped, and had shut his hand on all nine. What stood in front of them now was a fence of fire with their own road under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:97:She shut the door and let the peg fall. She did not take the stool. She carried the book to the sill, into the square of light, and laid it open on the strip of canvas among her own things, between the shears and the horn cup; and Cael thought that he had never seen an odder row.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:235:She had eaten, and had her two hours on her back, with the door shut and the book on the sill. She came out and washed to the elbow in the bucket, and she washed more slowly than she ever did, as a carter walks who thinks that his axle may be cracked and would sooner not hear it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:299:"Nobody," said Seln. "I walked the rise before we came down, and there is no rim to this place; you can see a mile of wet grass from here on every side. He may do as he thinks fit."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:97:"That is where the next mark would fall if the line ran straight," she said. "It won't. Watch."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:87:Cael had not known that he ever did. He walked across the yard in his slow dry way with a length of ash in his hand, peeled, as tall as his shoulder, with a fork left at the top of it for a palm to sit in, and he stood it against the trough where Lira's hand would find it. He kept his eyes off her foot; he lifted two fingers from his side, an inch, and let them fall, and went back up his ladder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:73:"It's that. The door's shut, and I'm on the wrong side of it from where I keep the thing." He let his arms fall. "It's queer. I've not used it since the ford, and I'd have told you I'd got used to going without. But I always knew where it was. I could feel it in the next room, as you can feel a fire through a wall. There's no next room."

