editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:349:He was the youngest of them. He had a book in front of him, shut on a pencil, and he sat quite still in the long light from the door with his hands on the cover; and he was not staring, as boys stared who wanted a place in a crew. He was reading. Teague could see the order of it. The boots. The wrist, and the leather on the wrist. The three in the doorway behind, one at a time, left to right. He was going over a stranger as Teague himself went over ground, and he was not troubling to hide it, any more than Teague was.
