editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:79:They slept that night in a halting-ring, with a fire of Seln's flint, and Cael sat his watch with Hesk's book shut on his knee. He owed a healer two readings of himself yet, on two different days, and he had not laid a finger on his own wrist since she told him *not tonight* between two bars of iron. He let it stand. She had asked for a night that was only a night; every one since had been the eve of a floor or the morrow of one, and he thought it likely that this was the eve of another. The debt weighed on him a good deal less than it had on a halting-ground above the wash, and he took that for a sign that he had begun to believe her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:141:"The edge of the Quieting," she said. "A third time, and no more warning in it than in the others."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:389:*Deployment since the Line: twenty-three.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:57:The woman in the cut had laid nineteen points round him while he fought her, each as fine as a fishing line, from fifteen feet, with the stillness of a person threading a needle. Whoever had laid these was forty paces off. And they were thick: every one was as thick as his wrist, and there were three where she would have used thirty. It was the same trade in a different hand, as a ship's cable and a stitch are the same trade: this hand threw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:33:"Ninety-sixth day. Dark. The west side of the stone. There were nine. Two are dead, one on the floor with a bolt in it that we'll not see again, and one there." He did not point. "Seven went out when the wall lit, north and south, along the outside of the line. I didn't see where they went after. Did anyone?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:91:The healer had not finished. She kept hold of the wrist, and she bent over Lira until her face was a hand's breadth away, and she spoke as she had spoken to Brom about a cuff, slowly, so that there should be no second meaning in any piece of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:189:*Ninety-sixth day, night. West of the stone, on the old road.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:223:*Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:257:"Later."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:261:"Later."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:273:*I'm not keeping it secret. I'm keeping it unfiled. There's a difference. Lira said later. Later is when I have a sentence. The answer might be: because whatever I am isn't a Path. I've known that since I was fourteen. Out there it was a flag. In there it's the only thing that works. I don't have a word for what that makes me, and I'm not going to invent one at a camp with Lira's arm two hours old.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:237:*Ninety-sixth day. The Fallow Ring, at the eastern lip of the floor. Sunset.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:237:*Quiet ground. Karis's Arbiter stopped at a line she can mark with a rock. So did Lira's, Brom's, Seln's, Oryn's — same line, same step. Mine didn't. Mine's been stopped since I was fourteen. I don't know what that means. Nobody asked. Tomorrow we go in.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:239:*The nine: held. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:35:He asked them in the yard, by the well, with the light gone grey. Brom looked at three boxes and then at his own left wrist, and Cael watched him weigh a road of six days against a rule of three, and put his hand up. Lira's was up already. Seln lifted two fingers from his folded arms. They had to wake Karis for hers, and she raised it before her eyes were properly open and asked afterward what she had voted for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:135:He walked behind the cart with his hands hanging, as he had walked behind another cart up a saddle, and every little while he turned his left wrist over and looked at the cuff. He ate his bread with the right hand. When the carter's off mule cast a shoe-nail in the middle of the morning and stood on three legs, it was Seln who held the hoof, and Brom stood by with both arms folded so tightly that the knuckles showed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:265:*The nine: held. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:357:He lay with two fingers on his wrist and counted what he could count. He knew where it lay, to the breadth of a finger. Nothing moved in it. And all ten went round it, and none through.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:389:*Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:141:Seln considered her, and then, with perfect gravity, he lifted his own right hand and shook it from the wrist, three times, with the fingers spread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:317:He wrote the day while Brom trimmed the wick. *Fifty-second day. Thornwater. Seven at the ford, by Seln's count, and one of them lies on the bar. Karis has a hypothesis, in two clauses, and tomorrow at dusk we test both. If I spend anything it will be Wind, once, where it can be seen. Deployment since the Line: none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:145:They went on. Cael walked the fourth cord with his head turned back over his shoulder. The bar stood in the grass where they had left it, brown and small, beside a square of black turf; and he thought that it was a strange thing to have crossed a Line, and a mountain, and ninety-six days of road, and to be met at the far end of all of it by two numerals that somebody at a counter had signed for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:155:He laid the short cord down all the same, since a wrist is not an instrument, and wrote what the knot said. He had written that figure nine times already.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:211:"A circle is a made shape."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:13:"One. A bar of iron of the registry's pattern, bearing its stamp and the number thirty-one, stands on the edge of the Quieting at the Fallow Ring, a boot's width from the place where I found that edge with my feet. Two. By its rust, and by the turf grown against it, it has stood there about five years. Three. It was driven at the far end of a measure, so that whoever drove it had found the same circle that I paced, and had a cord or a chain to find it with. Four. I do not know what the number counts, or what was written down afterward, or where it is kept."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:93:"The circle's perfect," she said. She was looking at the shut box. "Perfect isn't natural. Somebody made a floor that turns Paths off. It's older than anything I can date. And it doesn't turn you off. Three facts. I'm not putting a verb between them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:169:"Don't declare anything," said Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:217:*Nine. With the reading on the road, ten since Thornwater. Deployment since the Line: twenty-three.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:111:"They'll send people. A lure's no use if nobody's waiting at the end of it. People have faces. Six years I've wanted a face."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:195:*Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:29:They had gone out by the east at first light, as Seln had read off bare earth, and they had come home by the west, up the one track on the whole of that hillside which six people had made since noon. He thought of a healer at the far end of that track, counting to ten on a wrist, twice, with her Path running; he did not know that it bore, and he put it by for whoever kept findings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:173:Brom poured for her out of the skin. She washed to the wrists, rubbing hard, and it was while she did so that Cael read her; she had read him first, which did not often happen to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:275:"Twenty-seven days. I counted the stitches in and I counted them out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:281:"You pulled this week. I can feel where." She had her hands round the wrist already, and it took her a quarter of the time that the leg had taken. When she let go there was a seam on it, pink and dry, from the heel of the hand to the middle of the forearm, and it lay closed along its whole length. "That's two, and that's my day. You'll want telling what it's good for, and I'll tell you tomorrow, when I can think."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:283:She went to Karis last of the three, on her knees, and read her as she had read the leg, with two fingers on the inside of a thin wrist and two on the side of the neck. Her lips moved to ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:385:*Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:309:He wrote the day's entry short. *Fourteenth day. First line: six crossings stood, nothing lost. Earned, two. In hand, less than one, and a side of bacon. The reserve is not counted here and never will be. Thirteenth of thirteen, Lira says. Deployment since the Line: none. Pressure, Storm and Wind all offered when a tree came down the river, and Brom's Path offered too, and all four were told no.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:163:*Three. Deployment since the Line: twenty-six.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:203:*Read myself. Ten currents, and I can't find the banks either — Oryn's right. And the anomaly's there. Not a fragment. Not a flow. A still place, and the rest of me has been going around it since before I knew it existed. It isn't the Quiet. The Quiet is outside me and turns Paths off. This is inside me and the Paths — the fragments — go around it like they were built to. I don't know what it is. I know it was there before Oryn, before Brom's read, before the ring. Session nine caught the edge of it. I'm not going to guess. Guessing is what the registry did to me. Observation, then the log, then patience.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:227:"You're going to tell me about a thing that I'll never get my hands on. I'd take a finding from a second healer across a table, if I had felt the patient. I've not felt this one. I've had a river in the dark off you seven times, and that's all that I'll ever have." She spoke slowly, sorting it. "But you were in me for a minute, on that ridge, and I felt you go along. I know your step. If you're in me while you say it, I'll have the thing that found it, under my own skin, while it tells me what it found. It's the nearest that I'll come."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:237:"Yes. Hand on the wrist. The short reading first, to ten, and then the neck, and the long reading for one minute, which I'll count. You'll talk while you're in. If I say *out*, you come out with the sentence in your mouth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:263:"I gave it one a long while back, on an old leaf, when it was only something that another person's hands had caught the edge of. The still place. I never had anything to lay the words on till last night."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:275:"A still place that everything goes around. I've never felt that in anyone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:301:*Deployment since the Line: twenty-seven.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:281:*Deployment since the Line: none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:23:The hound struck at her right wrist. The knife turned in her fingers and went spinning onto the stones, and she went over backward after it with her head toward the water, and caught it up as she fell. He saw her hand shut on it, and saw that she had it by the wrong end, with the steel in her fist and the horn handle sticking out below; and the animal was already coming again, and she had no time to turn it round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:179:A man came out of the black water to his waist, and got one knee on the gravel's edge, streaming, with nothing whatever in his face. He had let his Path go while he was still under the surface, and it did not matter now, for every head on that river was turned to the place where the air had cracked. He moved once. It was a short and exact movement, under the jaw, and Cael had sat across a counter from that man for two years and had never guessed that he owned it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:419:*Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:19:"At that bench you asked to touch a boy while I had his wrist, so that you could feel what I felt. His leave was never yours to ask from me, and his treatment was not your lesson." She pushed back her left cuff. "Here you've asked to read me. This is my wrist, and I can answer for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:165:"Here," she said. "The edge of the Quieting. It is the same as the other; there is no warning in it at all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:181:It was the same pattern as the one that Cael had knelt by on the ninety-sixth day, a cord north of a grey stone: a square bar as thick as two fingers, with its head beaten down by a mallet. This one was scaled all over with rust, in leaves, so that it looked more like a thing grown than a thing made. A thumb's breadth under the head there was the registry's stamp, and under the stamp, punched deep, were two figures.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:281:*Deployment since the Line: fourteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:125:"It is," said Karis. "A band on each. I rubbed them on the afternoon of the ninety-sixth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:169:She began instead with what other people called it. A carter's wife had given her *quiet ground*, across a yard, in three sentences. A one-armed man had said the same two words under an eave, and had told them where the sigil went to sleep. She had those entered as said. They were good words for what they were, which was a warning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:193:*the Quieting*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:197:It was made out of a verb, like the other; it named a thing that went on happening, to whatever came over the line, for as long as it stayed there. It did not say how, and it did not say why. It took *the* in front, which made it one thing, and it would take a place after it without complaint: the Quieting at the Fallow Ring. She could write *inside* before it, and *the edge of*, and *a site of*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:247:"It's got a name," said Brom. "Quiet ground. The carter's wife said it, and the one-armed man, and that old keeper at the ford would have, I dare say, if we'd asked him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:257:"The Quieting." She made a face. "It sounds like what you do to a baby."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:91:*Compression, from Reydan. Only at contact; the load goes through me into the ground and the bill comes later. It charges nothing until something hits me.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:105:*Deployment since the Line: none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:243:*Twenty-fourth day. Second line: forty posts, cut and set. Four marks and flour. He said "Cheap," and Brom has kept the word. Earned, six. At dusk the sealed one took my left foot for a stride, on an empty road. Rent. Deployment since the Line: none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:77:*Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:105:"I counted it going in. Ten fours. I shan't count it again in front of him." She shut the purse with her elbow. "I shall do that later."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:341:"*Air cleared,*" said Karis. "He asked me what we'd done, and I told him, and he's written it down as though a person cleared air every day of the week." She had counted the four in. "Fifty-two."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:441:*Six lines in Pike's book, and the sixth is a well. Earned, sixty-two. In hand, fifty-two. Twelfth. The captain at the top has been in and gone out again on two holds' slips, and has said nothing to us or about us. Pike says that is the ledger.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:445:*In the middle box there are three sentences from a carter's wife about a thing she called the quiet ground, with two lines under two of the words. None of us knows what it is. Nobody has asked.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:451:*Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:7:There were fifty-two marks in the purse, and the name at the top of the sheet was still up the north water on two holds' slips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:203:*The nine: held. No Path has been lifted by any of us since the ford. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:51:He took his fingers off his wrist. The listening went on at the edge of his thought, quick and slow, and he let it go on, as a miller lets a mill; he would read himself on a night that was only a night, and until then the debt could stand where she had put it. It was the first that he had ever left standing without picking at it. He wrote one line, and gave the watch to Brom at the turn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:53:*Hundred and eleventh day. The halting-ground above the wash. One reading done, two owed, and nothing tried tonight; I was in a hurry, and she never asked for that. Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:165:He had been a number in other people's books for as long as he could remember. A magister had given him one every week, to the decimal. Vell had kept one for him in a yard. Every one of those figures had been somebody's best account of how much of a thing he was; and here was a book-keeper with one arm, who had never owned an instrument, saying aloud in a room that his own book had a true line in it and was too small to hold what the line meant. It was not a thing that any registry seat would have said. Cael did not know what to do with it, and he set it by, to be looked at later.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:225:The room let them go as it had let them come. The hide-woman's bundle was still between her feet, and the place that she had cleared on the bench end stood empty. The three grey men had not thrown. At the last bench a man with leather on his wrist watched a sheet of cream paper go out of the door in a boy's hand, and Cael could not have said what was in his face, since it was the face of somebody who had already done his own sum on that paper and did not mean to do another crew's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:149:"Their map has no roads. His has no quiet ground. Between them you get the country."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:255:Oryn had two fingers on her wrist already, and two on her neck, and her lips moved to ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:249:*Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:19:"The fork," said Seln. He had walked the first hours of that road by himself, in their second week at Lowmarch, and had never said what he found. "The left hand is ours, later. The right is the mill."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:105:"I've read one thing with what I had from you, and it was myself. I found no bank in it anywhere, as you found none. I don't know what a bank is like under the hand. I've only your word that there is such a thing." He kept his own hands at his sides. "When you take the boy's wrist, may I set my hand on it too? I want to learn what reaches you through yours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:153:"I was to be at this gate on the ninety-sixth. I came in on the hundred and twelfth. That's sixteen." The thumb went down. "There's a day's bench here still, that I'd have done tomorrow, and the road beyond it: two gates, and then the ford with the oxen on the hundred and twenty-first, where they looked for me on the hundred and fourth. That's seventeen." A finger. "Now put your ridge into it. It's a half-day up to wherever this is, and I'd not see the place before dark. A day while you're in it. A half-day down. That's two, if every one of you walks out on his own legs, and you've not once done that since I knew you." Another finger. "So the ford's nineteen. And beyond the ford there's a child that I heard on the eighty-ninth and can't hear now before the hundred and twenty-eighth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:207:*Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:97:"Nor I, and I watched one animal for a count of three hundred. They move as the hand of this clock moves." She rubbed her eyes with the back of her wrist. "One looks away, and it's later."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:129:It did so suddenly, and nothing on that shelf had moved so quickly in two days of his watching. Its off foreleg came up off the shelf and went down on the slope above, and the hind leg followed, and then both again, three steps, with a clatter of horn that reached him a moment later. Beside it the next did the same, and the next. He swept his eyes east along the shelf, and there was not one grey back in the whole line that was where it had been; all thirty had gone up the hill by the length of their own bodies, the calves scrambling inside them, and stood now on the open slope with their heads lifted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:61:Cael stood where he was. He had been a row on that leaf since noon on the ninety-sixth, ruled in Karis's hand at the foot of five others, with a numeral against it; and he had filled it for her himself, with a pencil going, in two halves that were each of them true.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:237:"A knife marks a stone. On the afternoon of the ninety-sixth, while Lira was pinning the fourth sheet, I put this point to the outside of that rim, low down on the north, where a scratch would spoil nothing, and I leaned on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:269:"It's older, I'll say once and not again, than [UNBOUND] — older than the word they retired, and I can't say by how much. Entered as a limit, not a finding. We explained the middle of the story in Ostrand. This is before the beginning, and I don't have a ruler for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:45:It was the third time he had been asked, in three different ways, and the first time the answer seemed to be put away somewhere for later. She took the slip from the carter's hat, where he had left it on the shaft. She read Pike's hand, slowly, and the five strokes under it, and then she fished a stub of charred stick out of her apron and made her mark on the bark, pressing hard. It was a thorn twig, three spines to a side, with a wavy line under it for water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:121:He had waited for the hour, and he went without his Path, as he had gone everywhere since a crossroads fire; Shadow was a declaration like any other, and he had told four people to make none. Nobody had given him the reason for the hour, and he had not sought it; but a carter of twenty-two years kept that rule, and so did a hold that had stood behind this fence for longer, and he had not lived to his age by supposing himself wiser than the people of a place. The man on the platform watched him lift the bar. He did not call down. Seln had the feeling of being entered in a column, correctly, by somebody who would not trouble him about it afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:223:*Deployment since the Line: none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:23:The arm was a scald, a week old, and it had gone bad. He saw a red line running up the inside of it from the wrist toward the elbow, and the woman's eyes were too bright. Oryn laid her on the ash and put both hands round the forearm above the cloth, and shut her eyes, and it was as it had been with Lira; there was nothing to see but a square brown woman breathing slowly, and the sweat coming out along her cropped hair. It took half an hour. When she lifted her hands the red line was a faint pink thread, and the woman on the table had begun to cry quietly from relief.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:73:"That's three times. The first I thought it was the break; a bad one can shout over everything. So I went where you're whole. There's nothing wrong with that wrist, or that neck, and there's still nothing I can read." She looked at Karis, who was the one writing it down, or would be. "It's like putting my hands in a river in the dark. Something's moving. I can't find the banks, so I can't read it, so I can't touch it. That's the rule. I didn't make it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:83:"They're the same thing, done twice as hard. That's the short one, and a healing house calls it the surface reading. Hands on the hurt, or on a wrist, and a count of ten. It tells me what's broken and where it's standing, and it costs me nothing I'd notice; I did eight of them along that bench." Oryn leaned on the edge of the ash. "There's a long one, which they call the deep reading. Both hands, and all of me, and it isn't a count, it's minutes. I go through the whole of how a person's built, as you'd go through every room of a house with a lamp. It costs what a mending costs. And the one under my hands has to lie still for it, and be whole, since a break shouts."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:259:The big one sat on the well's coping with a box-rope coiled over his knee. His left wrist had a cuff of clean linen on it, tied with a knot that Teague knew, since he had seen it on his own people. Mended, then, and under orders. The girl was on a trough. She had one boot, and the other foot was bare and strapped, crosswise, by somebody who had been shown how. It was on its whole sole. He had read those feet in an inn as the quickest thing in the room, and he would not have laid a copper on them now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:329:*The nine: held, on a mountain and off it. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:15:Then the walls, and here she was careful, since she had been greedy that morning and knew it. She had eleven sheets from the Fallow, where the bands ran round the feet of eleven stones, and twenty-nine from a room under the Stair. She had twelve from the hall. That made fifty-two, and the last twelve were all from one wall, rubbed upside down in an hour, by a person who was being held by the belt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:11:"Ninety-six in hand," said Brom, who had done it for her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:23:"Fifty-two," said Lira, from the door. She had counted them as they went down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:25:"Fifty-two sheets. Forty-one kinds, at the two that I could do properly. Three perimeters of one size, and a fourth that is twice it." Karis leaned on the deal with both palms. "I had three boxes of other people's stone when I crossed the Line. I should not have said that there was anything east of it to put in them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:31:*Hundred and twenty-ninth day, night. A back room with a door on it, in the lower inn at Lowmarch. First snow. Ninety-six in hand. Nine lines in Pike's book, and no number against the season. Eleventh on the cloth. Deployment since the Line: twenty-seven.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:39:*One anomaly. Session nine. Tide-adjacent. Not an entry any more; a location. The still place. Read three times. Doesn't flow. Doesn't change. Everything goes around it. Not the Quiet. Not a fragment. Older than the log. Observation continues.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:83:*Vell. There's a second ledger on the continent with my name in it, kept by a one-armed man in a town on no map, and this week he stopped numbering me. He said his book was wrong about us by being too small. You said once that somebody should have a record that doesn't answer to the Compact. There are two now. The session still stands. — C.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:251:"Someone made this pattern."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:255:"I have ten things that aren't a Path," Cael said, eventually, to nobody in particular, "no tier, no rank, no file I can see — and five people, one of them on a route, who've walked into the quiet three times and come out." He turned it over once and let it stand. "And a line on a map that somebody drew before there was a registry to draw one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:189:"The wrist. The one I did, that I read at the fire."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:211:"Is it Brom?" said Karis. "Forgive me; I must ask it. His Arbiter stopped at that stone, and yours did. If the thing that you read is his current, and his current is shaped by his Path, then perhaps there was nothing in him to read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:215:Brom stood on the western side of the stone, where he was an Iron Skin with his Path in the next room. He stretched out his left arm. Oryn kept her two hands on it, and she stepped over the stone by herself, so that she stood inside and he outside, with the mended wrist held between them across the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:301:One had come out of the mild man who stood six feet off with his hands behind him, looking east. It was the very thing that Seln had reached for at this stone, not half an hour ago, and not found; and Cael had held it shut at every dusk for ninety-six days, so that nobody who might one day speak of it to a carter bound for a ferry should know where a boy had learned it. A healer spoke to every carter on seven roads. She would not have meant the least harm by it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:201:Brom was looking at his left wrist. Lira had her eyes on the great boot, and on what was taped inside it; she had asked for that ankle once, and been told what it was worth and to whom it was owed. Seln studied his cup. And Cael had stood by a well with a sentence ready, six days ago, and had found that he had no right to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:219:*The nine: held. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:11:Cael gave it to them standing. He gave it as he had given it to one person on a gate-walk an hour before, in the same order and in fewer words: the noon of the ninety-sixth, and a read that had run across a stride, and a foot of Wind at a boulder; a row for a table that had been two true halves; and that he did not know why any of it was so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:61:"One: it is a thing that happens, and I have called it the Quieting. Two: five sigils of five Paths failed at one stride, in one way, and came back so. Three: the floor is older than the oldest stone that I can date. Four: the registry has put iron on the edge of it." She shut the notebook on her finger. "And there is a hypothesis, which is mine, and which is not entered anywhere."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:219:*Nothing out here reads me. And at a line you can mark with a rock, every Path in this camp stops, and mine doesn't. Karis won't write her hypothesis. I won't write mine. We're going to find the other circles. Pike said nobody goes in twice. Tomorrow makes three.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:155:He had said no at the first stride of every road for ninety-six days, and again at the second, on a bar of gravel and a hill of plates and a mile of grass with a grey thing pacing it. He had become so good at the refusal that he had taken it for what he was. He had kept the nine as a person keeps a purse shut; and he knew now what the purse had been kept for, and the relief of it was so sharp that it was nearly grief.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:301:It went up out of the grass along sixty yards, thirty to the north of the stone and thirty to the south, a wall of yellow-white fire as high as a man's knee and as straight as her wrist could draw a curve that size. He felt her discharge go past him like the door of a furnace. It was the first thing that anybody but himself had declared on that hillside since noon, and after what he had carried down the slope it seemed to him a clean and ordinary thing, like bread.

