+
+### Movement 5 — chapters 30–37
+
+- Calendar holds from the Ring arrival on day ninety-five through the long reading on day one hundred: day ninety-seven is logged at 35:51–59; day ninety-eight begins at 35:119–145; day ninety-nine is explicit at 35:265–271; the reading begins at first light after that at 36:233–279.
+- Ring geometry is internally legible: the floor is twenty-six yard-strides across and ringed by eleven uprights/bands (30:57–61); the den is under the eastern lip (30:177–181); the western line stone is named as 220 yards from the floor's middle (30:211–217). The tactical retreat begins 207 yards from the western stone after crossing the floor, consistent with the stated scale (32:3–9).
+- The Ring deployment is coherent and the rules stay differentiated: Shadow is used once, unseen by Oryn and recognized only by Seln (32:117–127; 34:25–47); Storm's three lanes bill ten, ten, and twelve counts (32:209–225); Anchor finds nine bodies at roughly ten feet (32:279–285); Ember is a point, Wind locks the landing for one count, Compression takes contact, and Blade remains partial (32:139–249). The audit logs twelve Ring deployments plus the ford's one, for thirteen total (33:189–233).
+- Bodies and stores hold: Lira's bitten forearm is mended but kept in a sling until first snow (33:91–119); her ankle is explicitly left alone (35:79–81, 155–167). Brom has four new finger-pads and five recoverable bolts, with the sixth left on the Ring floor (34:113–151; 35:15–23). Karis's throat recovers one word at a time (35:241–251; 36:23–27).
+- The eleven Ring sheets are taken without pretending to decode the work (30:121–145). Seln's remembered Compact map supports correspondence but not cause or construction (34:51–109, 227–249); “Compact” is spoken only in that authorized discussion (34:247–249).
+- The consent breach is unmistakable at Reader Standard. Beforehand Cael says Oryn cannot consent (36:65–77), admits that the real reason for silence is fear she will refuse (36:99–115), hears Brom, Karis, Seln, and Lira call it wrong and distinguish reading him from permitting a taking (36:119–183), then chooses it anyway (36:185–197). His written record says he chose the cost for her and that recording it does not make it allowed (36:201–225). At the instant, he knows he can stop, knows stopping is right, and consciously reaches instead (37:141–171). Nothing here reads as retroactive consent.
+- Oryn detects the change at once and Cael begins payment with the truth on the table, not later in private (37:202–240). Her staying to hear him is not yet forgiveness; it is a healer requiring the patient to remain still.
+- The book's core argument sharpens cleanly: inside the line Cael is “the only thing that worked” (36:111–115), while Oryn's reading makes him a patient rather than a case (37:95–111). Those gains are deliberately contaminated by his choice to take.
+- Reader Standard: the Ring attack is frightening and exact, but the emphasis stays on positions, choices, and injuries rather than gore (31:211–289; 32:3–321). The long reading's house/river imagery explains an abstract power without requiring system vocabulary (37:45–91, 121–137).
+- Repetition watch: “nobody” silences cluster after the Ring (35:23, 49, 221; 36:11, 113, 183); counting is both plot mechanism and prose habit throughout chapters 35–37; “as a person/man” comparisons accumulate (35:207, 267; 37:5, 33, 47, 107, 133).
+
+### Movement 6 — chapters 38–45
+
+- Calendar holds: the telling/read occurs on day 100 (38:77–87, 191–203); day 101 is grain/practice (39:121–135, 245–249); birthday day 102 (40:3–5, 53–67, 189–209); research days 103–110 (41:3–5, 65–93, 103–143, 163–209; 42:3–15, 79–125, 205–209, 283–299; 43:3–15, 57–69, 115–153); Oryn and crew leave north/west on day 111 (44:225–247; 45:3–15); Lowmarch is day 112 (45:57–61).
+- The telling respects every knowledge boundary. Oryn learns the plate, [UNBOUND] without its retired word, all ten fragments, and the Ring behavior from Cael, not another person (38:3–47). He names “the Bronze from Ardenmere” in the list (38:23–25), protects Shadow's source, and states that nobody knows why the fragments answer inside (38:25–31). Teague is neither told nor read.
+- The consent/debt thread remains morally exact. Oryn identifies the theft as taking her asking and reversing her table (38:103–125, 191–229); she explicitly refuses both retroactive pardon and Cael's attempt to set his own fine (38:131–177). Continued medical care, birthday attendance, and curiosity are each kept distinct from forgiveness (38:269; 40:137–147, 189–217). The outward rule becomes “ask first, every time,” then adds the necessary purpose test—not reading a willing friend merely to see whether the instrument works (39:281–291; 44:145–165, 197–213).
+- The Tide fragment's behavior is clear: it listens without asking, reads self and contact only, finds ten flows without banks, and detects a motionless place that is neither a fragment nor decoded (38:273–299; 40:325–379). The text does not convert that discovery into a causal answer.
+- Deployment remains thirteen. Eleven tiny Wind practice leans in a fenced yard are explicitly entered but excluded by the established field-use definition (39:245–249); the day-102 inventory also reports thirteen (40:383–389).
+- Money holds: 108 before the six-mark birthday room/kid, leaving 102 (40:23–37, 65–67, 383–389). Pike's second posting offers 300 but is not yet accepted (45:177–199).
+- Sheets and research stay disciplined: all eleven Ring rubbings are laid flat (41:3–39); Karis refuses to infer a sign inventory from one site (41:43–95); “the Quieting” is not named until she creates and audits the term (41:163–203, 237–291); [UNBOUND] appears once as the limit on dating (42:251–271). Her hypothesis is acknowledged but never stated, including in the private exchange with Cael (43:57–111).
+- Maker limits hold. The defensible statement is only that somebody made the perfect floor which turns Paths off (43:89–105); no method, identity, or purpose is supplied. The registry bar remains a dated/stamped observation whose “31” is not decoded (43:3–53).
+- Lira's body arc is careful: the forearm's eighth mend is slow but takes by day 110; use remains forbidden until snow (42:79–125; 43:115–143). The tibia may run after a month, while the ankle remains taped, flat-footed, and unmended (43:137–143; 45:11–21). Her one-handed bow work grows through constraint rather than a miraculous recovery (42:129–201, 283–343; 43:213–259).
+- Oryn leaves the mule at Thornwater for the crew and walks north on day 111, preserving owner decision #24 and the route arithmetic (43:157–201; 44:225–261). Her own body/reserve is intact beyond the known mending cost; eleven later mendings confirm nothing came back short (44:265–295).
+- Pike's line and wall logic hold: the Fallow completion line is spoken twice, says six in/six out/no price, and moves the crew from tenth to eleventh with Teague ten above (45:105–165). The board's inadequacy adds a social measure that the ranking cannot express, rather than reversing the ranking.
+- Arc: the birthday on day 102 supplies belonging without acquittal (40:129–217). Karis's one-basket restraint and Oryn's three-readings rule repeat the same epistemic ethic in different trades (41:43–95; 44:175–213), giving the post-Ring recovery movement a coherent argument rather than mere decompression.
+- Repetition watch: “as a person/man” similes are extremely dense (38:69, 209, 221, 229, 235, 251, 295; 39:73, 95, 133, 141, 229; 40:23, 35, 147, 287; 41:67; 42:57; 43:53). Counting/accounting diction remains omnipresent, often intentionally. “A stride and a half” repeats as Oryn's stage direction (38:53; 39:265; 41:103; 43:149). The explanatory move “That was...” also continues (38:223–225; 39:135–147; 40:49–55; 41:85–93).
+- Pacing watch: chapters 41–43 deliver five findings in extended procedural scenes. The method is character-bearing and the one-basket correction pays off, but this is the book's clearest reflective plateau after the Ring; straight through, the third/fourth findings approach re-proof rather than forward motion (42:205–279; 43:3–53). The Stair posting at 45:177–225 restores propulsion.
+
+### Movement 7 — chapters 46–53
+
+- Calendar holds: Lowmarch decision night day 112 (46:187–195, 259–263); Millrace and Stair arrival day 113 (47:3–25, 121–153; 48:127–171, 273–281); trap day 114 (49:3–19; 51:193–217); survey day 115 (52:3–21, 355–359); travel days 116–118 and Hall arrival day 118 (53:59–103).
+- Stair geometry is consistent: the Quieting edge is forty paces inside the carter ring; the floor is 220 yards from the line stone (49:3–5). The floor is about thirty yards across; the stair is forty steps; its room has five courses per wall and a roof only a hand above Brom (49:159–193; 52:61–101). The faint/clear/absent Anchor bands are exact at ten/twelve/fourteen feet (50:261–267).
+- The trap's cause-and-effect is readable in one pass: a bolt draws Oryn five yards inside, three anchored courses close behind her, six observers maintain a rim, and cold/Brom's untreated wound are meant to force surrender (49:21–83, 87–121; 50:105–123). The attack is frightening and exact, not lurid; the bolt wound is described anatomically only to explain why it stays in until Tide returns (49:237–273).
+- Shadow's second and final use occurs from the fourth stair step to the old bar, entirely outside hostile lines of sight; Lira sees only its disappearance from below and Seln deliberately turns away (50:173–181, 213–255; 51:193–201). Oryn is underground and never learns its source. The usage is not attributable to a watcher (51:165–173).
+- Fragment rules hold: Wind locks one count (51:21–33); Storm's one lane bills ten counts (51:37–49, 207–213); Compression takes the crossbow blow at contact (51:51–59); Ember is one point (51:73–83); Anchor unbinds at touch (50:281–295); Tide requires the person's consent and physical contact (47:97–123; 48:11–53, 73–105). Deployment advances from thirteen to fourteen with the consensual Tide read (48:273–281), then nine Stair uses make twenty-three (51:193–217).
+- Consent reaches its necessary test at Millrace: Cael asks to read the boy and accepts Oryn's no immediately (47:97–129); later he asks Oryn a different question, receives her explicit yes, follows surface/long limits, and exits at her word (48:7–27, 29–53, 73–105). The prose explicitly identifies body ownership—the boy's leave was not Oryn's to give; her wrist is hers (48:17–23). There is no forgiveness language attached to the yes.
+- The route/crew distinction also holds: Oryn refuses membership, chooses to put them on her route, and sets the terms herself (47:145–225). Her later continuation through Brom's week and the next edge is her own counted decision, not a request coerced from her (53:37–55).
+- Bodies hold: Brom's bolt crosses the right shoulder, misses lung/major vessel, remains as a plug until Tide returns, then is drawn and mended for an hour (49:237–273; 51:125–153). Lira's ankle gives on step twenty and the forearm reopens at two newest points; the arm is rebound and mended, still barred from use (49:281–301; 51:143–153). Cael's right forearm takes the crossbow strike through Compression and his left palm is burned by Ember (51:47–83, 157–163). Oryn empties three times at Millrace, then spends two more mendings after the Stair (47:43–85; 51:125–153).
+- Names remain controlled: the six on the rim are never named or unmasked; Teague's three remain only bow/spear/boy, and Teague never asks for Cael's mechanism or receives a fragment inventory (51:225–255, 259–383). “The Bronze from Ardenmere” does not arise in a spoken list in this movement.
+- The Stair adds to the Ring. The Ring established identical suppression and Cael's exception; the Stair weaponizes those facts, proves the registry has resurveyed a known site, exposes the lure as a pre-restriction inventory, and forces the exception to be used under secrecy constraints (46:3–47; 48:175–221; 49:21–121; 50:143–181). It is not merely a second demonstration.
+- The Hall also begins by adding a third site on a measured bearing, not simply repeating a boundary: the survey ledger ends “third site confirmed on bearing” (52:177–239); the mapped line reaches the drowned hall within the admitted error of a map made in days (53:3–35, 95–119); site 33 lacks the later resurvey bar/mark (53:127–157); the submerged organism responds to staying weight, creating a new physical rule (53:191–243, 247–339).
+- The protected reserved truths hold: the stroke-and-bar mark is copied but not decoded (48:205–217; 52:139–159, 187–203); the surveyor's four-sign index is not identified (52:177–203); the Stair case is recovered but Seln's locked case remains shut. Only the authorized maker sentence is made: the floor carving knows the circle's size (52:291–311).
+- Movement 7's extra length is earned. It contains the contract's moral debate, the first refused request and first consented outward read, two-day Stair siege/resolution, Teague's independent arrival, the survey, the bearing reveal, three days of travel, and the third site's rule test. Each stage changes the available knowledge or an ethical rule (46:51–185; 47:97–225; 49:21–121; 50:143–209; 51:225–255; 52:177–311; 53:191–339). It does not read padded, though the full Teague flashback after the Stair climax delays release (51:259–383).
+- Repetition watch: the book's “counting” texture peaks here—distances, observers, bolts, consent, mendings, deployment, and route days all use the same diction. “He had never...” constructions cluster in the decision and trap aftermath (46:111–123, 153–161, 273–285; 47:115–123; 51:179–187). Hands are both subject and stage business in nearly every major exchange.
+
+### Movement 8 — chapters 54–60
+
+- Calendar holds: Hall entry day 119 (54:3–15; 55:153–165); three readings completed and telling on day 120 (55:169–219, 295–301); four-site argument/departure day 121 (57:91–149); Oryn turns north day 122 (58:3–63); return begins day 124, first snow and Lowmarch day 129 (59:3–15, 47–49, 121–139); last ferry day 130 (60:47–95); Oryn's letter arrives day 131 (60:219–225).
+- Hall adds a new rule and a new cost rather than replaying the Ring/Stair: its blind organism hunts weight that stays, abandons its round once it has a target, and can be diverted by a deliberately harder Anchor (53:229–339; 54:91–107, 159–187). The rescue uses ordinary learned movement, Compression at contact, Anchor at a point, and Lira's one-handed shooting as a coordinated solution (54:191–265, 267–323).
+- Violence stays intense but non-lurid. Brom's reopened shoulder, Cael's old ribs, and the living creature's injury are described through function, choice, and treatment; the animal is explicitly left alive (54:139–175, 191–241, 251–303; 55:3–31, 49–107, 153–165).
+- Final body continuity holds. Brom's right shoulder is mended a second time and may not be mended a third this cold term; the non-Path binder adds a four-yard drover's web (55:25–45; 56:33–67). Lira's forearm remains in its high sling and is not released merely because snow falls; ankle remains taped/flat and never mended (58:17–27; 59:23–45, 121–129). Cael's old ribs speak for ten days and clear on day 129 (55:89–103; 59:19–21, 47–49). Oryn's reserve is full after three days without mending, then spends two at Thornwater (60:109–119, 147–175).
+- Consent debt is fully paid without forgiveness. Cael completes three self-readings, tells Oryn first and all at once, and receives a second explicit yes before reading her during the telling (55:169–219, 221–271). Oryn marks that performance debt paid while keeping the original taking where she put it (55:275–287); her later POV says plainly that she has not forgiven him (60:121–125). At Teague's wrist Cael does not read and does not even ask, because curiosity alone is not a purpose and Teague's guarded information is his to offer (56:153–205). This final “wrist not read” makes asking the person whose body it is a lived rule, not just a vow.
+- “The still place” knowledge boundary holds: it is Cael's old private label until he tells Oryn (55:193–203, 243–279), then tells the crew after her (57:191–211). It is explicitly distinguished from the Quieting and never given a cause (55:195–203, 257–279; 60:35–39).
+- Three identical smaller sites establish perimeter, radius, forty-one sign kinds, and the lone radius-divided line; the fourth is paced only from outside, never entered, left unnamed, and is twice the size with a registry cordon (56:3–17; 57:13–23, 57–79, 91–125; 58:117–161, 165–225). The ending's “four sites, one line” is warranted as map alignment, not equivalence of interiors (58:225–229; 60:237–265).
+- Protected maker limit holds: the only final maker sentence is “Someone made this pattern” (60:247–253), supported by four aligned sites but not expanded into who, why, or how. The preceding thought remains an inference, not spoken fact (60:247–251).
+- Names/roles hold: the fourth site receives no name; Teague's people remain bow/spear/boy/old woman; the Hall creature gets only a carter's folklore label in reported speech, not an authoritative naming (53:117–123; 56:19–25, 71–109; 58:125–139).
+- Money closes exactly: 102 remains through the Stair (52:355–359), then six marks purchase the Lowmarch paper room, leaving 96 in hand (60:3–13, 29–31). The 300 remains taken/not paid; no contract money is added (59:161–175).
+- Board/sheets close exactly: Pike records nine lines and leaves the season's rating blank while the wall remains eleventh (59:175–207, 211–217; 60:29–31). Sheets total eleven Ring + twenty-nine Stair + twelve Hall = fifty-two (57:13–17; 60:19–25).
+- Exit lands. The book moves from “nothing out here reads me” to freedom complicated by his unique function, then to an oriented search governed by consent and shared evidence (58:99–113, 219–229; 60:31–43, 247–265). The final sentence stops at the defensible boundary: maker/pattern, not motive. It hands Book 8 a direction, a thaw deadline, Teague's alliance, Oryn's return condition, and an unopened fourth site without relying on Book 8's unreconciled plan (59:219–225; 60:215–265).
+- Repetition watch: “as he/she had...” retrospective comparisons remain very dense and repeatedly pull present action back through prior scenes (54:3–29, 91–107, 159–175; 55:21–29, 61–75, 169–203; 58:95–113; 60:69–83). “A person...” generalizations recur as explanatory codas (55:113–119, 137–151; 56:47–61, 173–181; 60:101–125). Counting, hands, bowls, and “entered/noted” deliberately close motifs but create a highly patterned surface.

