+### Movement 5 — chapters 30–37
+
+- Calendar holds from the Ring arrival on day ninety-five through the long reading on day one hundred: day ninety-seven is logged at 35:51–59; day ninety-eight begins at 35:119–145; day ninety-nine is explicit at 35:265–271; the reading begins at first light after that at 36:233–279.
+- Ring geometry is internally legible: the floor is twenty-six yard-strides across and ringed by eleven uprights/bands (30:57–61); the den is under the eastern lip (30:177–181); the western line stone is named as 220 yards from the floor's middle (30:211–217). The tactical retreat begins 207 yards from the western stone after crossing the floor, consistent with the stated scale (32:3–9).
+- The Ring deployment is coherent and the rules stay differentiated: Shadow is used once, unseen by Oryn and recognized only by Seln (32:117–127; 34:25–47); Storm's three lanes bill ten, ten, and twelve counts (32:209–225); Anchor finds nine bodies at roughly ten feet (32:279–285); Ember is a point, Wind locks the landing for one count, Compression takes contact, and Blade remains partial (32:139–249). The audit logs twelve Ring deployments plus the ford's one, for thirteen total (33:189–233).
+- Bodies and stores hold: Lira's bitten forearm is mended but kept in a sling until first snow (33:91–119); her ankle is explicitly left alone (35:79–81, 155–167). Brom has four new finger-pads and five recoverable bolts, with the sixth left on the Ring floor (34:113–151; 35:15–23). Karis's throat recovers one word at a time (35:241–251; 36:23–27).
+- The eleven Ring sheets are taken without pretending to decode the work (30:121–145). Seln's remembered Compact map supports correspondence but not cause or construction (34:51–109, 227–249); “Compact” is spoken only in that authorized discussion (34:247–249).
+- The consent breach is unmistakable at Reader Standard. Beforehand Cael says Oryn cannot consent (36:65–77), admits that the real reason for silence is fear she will refuse (36:99–115), hears Brom, Karis, Seln, and Lira call it wrong and distinguish reading him from permitting a taking (36:119–183), then chooses it anyway (36:185–197). His written record says he chose the cost for her and that recording it does not make it allowed (36:201–225). At the instant, he knows he can stop, knows stopping is right, and consciously reaches instead (37:141–171). Nothing here reads as retroactive consent.
+- Oryn detects the change at once and Cael begins payment with the truth on the table, not later in private (37:202–240). Her staying to hear him is not yet forgiveness; it is a healer requiring the patient to remain still.
+- The book's core argument sharpens cleanly: inside the line Cael is “the only thing that worked” (36:111–115), while Oryn's reading makes him a patient rather than a case (37:95–111). Those gains are deliberately contaminated by his choice to take.
+- Reader Standard: the Ring attack is frightening and exact, but the emphasis stays on positions, choices, and injuries rather than gore (31:211–289; 32:3–321). The long reading's house/river imagery explains an abstract power without requiring system vocabulary (37:45–91, 121–137).
+- Repetition watch: “nobody” silences cluster after the Ring (35:23, 49, 221; 36:11, 113, 183); counting is both plot mechanism and prose habit throughout chapters 35–37; “as a person/man” comparisons accumulate (35:207, 267; 37:5, 33, 47, 107, 133).
