books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-24.md:133:"I have ten things that aren't a Path," Cael said, eventually, to nobody in particular, "no tier, no rank, no file I can see — and five people, one of them on a route, who've walked into the quiet three times and come out." He turned it over once and let it stand. "And a line on a map that somebody drew before there was a registry to draw one."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-23.md:37:"Thornwater's that way," she said. "Four days, by that gap. I'm three weeks late there, and three weeks is three weeks, and there's an old woman at Thornwater whose knee I said I'd see, and I said it in the spring." She had the mule's rope in her hand and the roll on her back and her face square to the north-west track, which was how she stood at everything. "I've been a stop on my own route for nine days. I'm going back to being the route."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-17.md:121:"Inside the circle, I'm the only one who works. They chose a floor that makes the rest of you helpless. They don't know it doesn't do anything to me."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-20.md:107:"This one. It's not on the Ring. I looked for it for four days and it isn't on any upright. It's here, on every wall, and it's not in the courses with the rest — it's *between* them. On its own. Here." The pencil moved along the sheet to the next one, and the next. "And here. And here. I've paced the spacing because I pace things. It comes every — " she looked at the notebook, where she had written it, and read it out as a number she had checked twice — "every ten cords and a piece. Two hundred meters and a stride, to the stride. It recurs, on the wall, at the perimeter's radius."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-16.md:155:Vastin received the broker's completion report as a courtesy copy, forwarded through a registry office that had no interest in any board east of the Line, in the second folder of the morning, marked *Fallow Ring: crew returned, instruments not recovered*. He read it twice — a broker's report from a town on no registry map, on his desk with no routing note — and did not know why it had reached him, and filed that, in the register of a man who had never once in forty years pretended to knowledge he lacked, as the whole of what he knew about it. He returned the copy to its folder unannotated.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-13.md:113:*Written before. Tomorrow morning, the back room, the door shut. Oryn runs the long reading. Both hands, all of her, all of me, twenty minutes. I lie still. I'm still and whole for the first time in front of her; she said so in the yard, with the tape off.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-13.md:171:"Everyone I've ever had my hands in, this is the part where I know." She said it as a finding about herself. "Iron Skin, you feel the banks first — dense, walled, everything held. Ember, the banks are a fire's, hot at the edge. Wind you barely feel the banks at all, the water's too fast. But there's always a bank. It's how you know which river." A pause, long, her current going along Ember and stopping at the ignition point and lying there as if it had found a stone in a bed. "I can't find one here. I keep putting my hand where the bank ought to be and there's more water."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-11.md:221:"Now the rest, because I've not got it in me to say it twice." Oryn's voice was level and had nothing left under it. "A mend that deep holds nothing for a season. Nothing. That arm is not to hold. It's not to strike. It's not to catch. Not a rope, not a knife, not a fall, not a friend. Not until I say. And I'll say at first snow, near enough, if it's let alone." She took Lira's left wrist and laid the arm across her chest, and bound it there with the linen, high, a sling from the shoulder. "One arm. From here to the snow."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md:47:*Weather first, because you'll want it in order, and I've watched you want things in order.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md:171:"Teague's nine up," Pike said, to the book, asked by nobody. Then, still to the book, at the same size, as if it were the next line and not a different thing: "He read that slip yesterday. Read it twice, same as the boy did. Put it back under the stone and took the fence. I'd have done the same, and I told him, and he said he knew."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-12.md:67:The hand was the pads of four fingers laid open by a string in the dark, bled into the linen and dried there, and she soaked the linen off with the pan's water and a corner of the lantern's oil, slowly, while Brom looked at the scrub. When it was off she turned the hand over in the light and said, "String," as a verdict, and put her palms round it.

