   100	
   101	Teague stood a breath longer in front of two slips with five names on each.
   102	
   103	They would be at the ferry tomorrow. He had kept a day ahead of them from Oxhollow without trying to, and he knew their pace, with a taped boy and a girl on one boot. The boy would read this plank as he had read a doorway, from one end to the other, leaving nothing out. He would find the stone.
   104	
   105	Teague had said four things to him on a river bank, and every one of them had been counted before it was spoken. This was not a thing that could be counted. A captain did not walk up to another crew and hand it a feeling, as though a feeling were a figure; if he had, he would have been asked what it rested on, and rightly, and he would have had no answer but the hair on his own neck.
   106	
   107	So he said nothing, and it sat poorly with him. He went up the bank to find the boy, and whatever was in the pot.
